# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Class 5 Mathematics Chapter 8: Weight and Capacity - Master Data"""

slides = [{'id': 1, 'title': 'Weight Estimation', 'tagline': 'Check! Check! • Estimating Real-World Masses & Reading Scales', 'lecture': 'Welcome to the study of weight and capacity! In daily life, we constantly interact with objects of very different masses, from a light spoon to a heavy steel wardrobe. Estimation is our mental superpower for evaluating whether a measured quantity makes practical physical sense before we even touch a scale. Consider the common items found in a home: a sturdy steel almirah weighs around 80 kilograms, a solid wooden bed weighs about 40 kilograms, a standard sack of basmati rice weighs 25 kilograms, and a two-seater sofa weighs close to 35 kilograms. In contrast, smaller everyday items are much lighter: an empty plastic bucket weighs approximately 1 kilogram, while a full one-litre water bottle also weighs roughly 1 kilogram because one litre of water has a mass of exactly one thousand grams. A large double-door refrigerator weighs around 60 kilograms. If someone claims a water bottle weighs 50 kilograms or an almirah weighs only 200 grams, our estimation alarm immediately rings! To measure mass precisely, we use weighing instruments. Analog scales use internal springs that stretch under load, rotating a needle across a circular dial marked with kilogram graduations and fine gram subdivisions. Digital scales rely on electronic strain-gauge load cells that convert mechanical pressure into crisp digital numbers. By checking estimation first, we avoid costly measurement blunders.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🏠</span> Household Mass Benchmarks\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Developing an intuitive sense of mass prevents gross calculation errors. Compare typical real-world items against standard metric values:\n                </p>\n                <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 text-xs">\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">🚪 Steel Almirah</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">~80 kg</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">🛏️ Wooden Bed</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">~40 kg</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">🌾 Rice Bag</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">25 kg</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">🛋️ Living Room Sofa</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">~35 kg</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">🧊 Refrigerator</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">~60 kg</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">🪣 Empty Plastic Bucket</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-amber-400">~1 kg</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>⚖️</span> Analog vs Digital Weighing Scales\n                    </h4>\n                    <ul class="text-xs text-slate-300 space-y-2 leading-relaxed">\n                        <li class="flex items-start gap-2">\n                            <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold">•</span>\n                            <span><strong>Analog Dial Scale:</strong> An internal spring expands under gravitational force. A pointer needle rotates clockwise along dial tick marks (major intervals of 1 kg, minor tick marks of 100 g or 50 g).</span>\n                        </li>\n                        <li class="flex items-start gap-2">\n                            <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold">•</span>\n                            <span><strong>Digital Load Cell:</strong> Piezo-resistive strain gauges detect minuscule bending and convert physical compression directly into an electronic LCD readout with 1 g precision.</span>\n                        </li>\n                        <li class="flex items-start gap-2">\n                            <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold">•</span>\n                            <span><strong>Zero-Tare Function:</strong> Always verify that an empty pan indicates exactly 0 kg / 0 g before loading any objects to eliminate container weight bias.</span>\n                        </li>\n                    </ul>\n                </div>\n                <div class="mt-3 p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-cyan-950/40 border border-cyan-800/40 text-[11px] text-cyan-200">\n                    💡 <strong>Reasonableness Rule:</strong> If an answer seems wildly too light or too heavy for an object\'s physical volume, re-check the unit immediately!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c1_1', 'question': 'Which of the following recorded masses for a household object is completely UNREASONABLE and incorrect?', 'options': ['A family refrigerator weighing 62 kg', 'A standard school water bottle weighing 55 kg', 'A sack of whole wheat flour weighing 10 kg', 'An empty plastic bucket weighing 850 g'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Think about whether a ten-year-old child could lift a school water bottle filled with drinking water.', 'explanation': 'A school water bottle holds around 750 mL to 1 L of water, giving it a total mass of roughly 1 kg. A recorded mass of 55 kg equals the weight of a grown adult, which is physically impossible for a handheld water bottle!'}, {'id': 'c1_2', 'question': 'An analog spring scale has dial markings where 0 kg to 1 kg is divided into 10 equal divisions. If the pointer rests on the 7th tick mark past 3 kg, what is the weight?', 'options': ['3 kg 7 g', '3 kg 70 g', '3 kg 700 g', '37 kg'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': '1 kg = 1,000 g. Find the value of one division: 1,000 g ÷ 10.', 'explanation': 'Since 1 kg = 1,000 g, each of the 10 divisions represents 1,000 ÷ 10 = 100 g. The 7th tick mark past 3 kg represents 3 kg + (7 × 100 g) = 3 kg 700 g (or 3,700 g).'}]}, {'id': 2, 'title': 'Weight Equivalence', 'tagline': 'Different Units but Same Measure • Double Number Lines for kg and g', 'lecture': 'In the international metric system, the prefix kilo originates from the Greek word chilioi, which means exactly one thousand. Therefore, one kilogram is precisely equivalent to one thousand grams. This decimal relationship makes metric conversions remarkably systematic and predictable. To visualize this continuous equivalence, mathematicians use a double number line. On a double number line, two parallel calibrated number axes run side by side. The upper axis tracks mass in kilograms, while the lower axis tracks the exact same mass in grams. When the top marker points to zero kilograms, the bottom points to zero grams. When the top advances halfway to zero point five kilograms, which is half a kilogram, the bottom marker points directly to five hundred grams. Similarly, one quarter of a kilogram equals two hundred and fifty grams, three quarters of a kilogram aligns with seven hundred and fifty grams, and one full kilogram lines up perfectly with one thousand grams. Extending further, one and a half kilograms equals one thousand five hundred grams, two kilograms equals two thousand grams, and two and a quarter kilograms equals two thousand two hundred and fifty grams. Even though the numbers and unit symbols change, the physical measure remains identical. Double number lines clarify fraction and decimal relationships intuitively.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>📏</span> The Double Number Line Principle\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    A double number line maps equivalent quantities on two synchronized axes. Notice how standard fractional kilogram benchmarks match specific gram totals:\n                </p>\n                <div class="space-y-2 text-xs">\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300 font-medium">1/4 kg (Quarter kg)</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-400">➔</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">250 g</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300 font-medium">1/2 kg (Half kg)</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-400">➔</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">500 g</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300 font-medium">3/4 kg (Three-quarter kg)</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-400">➔</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">750 g</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300 font-medium">1 kg (One Whole kg)</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-400">➔</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">1,000 g</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300 font-medium">1 1/2 kg (One and a half kg)</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-400">➔</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">1,500 g</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>⚖️</span> Golden Equivalence Axiom\n                    </h4>\n                    <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-gradient-to-r from-amber-500/10 via-cyan-500/10 to-indigo-500/10 border border-amber-500/30 text-center my-3">\n                        <div class="text-2xl font-black text-amber-300 tracking-wide">1 kg = 1,000 g</div>\n                        <div class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-1">1 g = 1/1,000 kg = 0.001 kg</div>\n                    </div>\n                    <ul class="text-xs text-slate-300 space-y-2 leading-relaxed">\n                        <li class="flex items-start gap-2">\n                            <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">✓</span>\n                            <span><strong>Multiply by 1,000:</strong> To convert pure kilograms into grams, multiply the kilogram figure by 1,000 (shift decimal 3 places right).</span>\n                        </li>\n                        <li class="flex items-start gap-2">\n                            <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">✓</span>\n                            <span><strong>Divide by 1,000:</strong> To convert pure grams into kilograms, divide the gram figure by 1,000 (shift decimal 3 places left).</span>\n                        </li>\n                    </ul>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-indigo-950/40 border border-indigo-800/40 text-[11px] text-indigo-200 mt-3">\n                    📌 <strong>Memory Key:</strong> 1 kilogram is a thousand times heavier than 1 gram. Think of a 1 kg bag of sugar balanced by 1,000 individual 1-gram plastic counters!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c2_1', 'question': 'A fruit vendor places a 3/4 kg iron weight on one pan of a balance beam. How many grams of grapes must be placed on the other pan to make it perfectly level?', 'options': ['340 g', '750 g', '500 g', '1,750 g'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': '1 kg = 1,000 g. What is 3/4 of 1,000 g?', 'explanation': '3/4 of 1,000 g is calculated as (1,000 ÷ 4) × 3 = 250 × 3 = 750 g. So 3/4 kg is exactly equivalent to 750 g.'}, {'id': 'c2_2', 'question': 'On a double number line connecting kilograms and grams, which point aligns directly underneath 2 1/4 kg?', 'options': ['2,140 g', '2,250 g', '2,500 g', '2,750 g'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': '2 1/4 kg = 2 kg + 1/4 kg. Convert both parts to grams.', 'explanation': '2 kg = 2,000 g and 1/4 kg = 250 g. Adding them gives 2,000 g + 250 g = 2,250 g.'}]}, {'id': 3, 'title': 'Weight Conversions', 'tagline': 'Let Us Find • Decomposing Compound Units & The 3-Digit Gram Rule', 'lecture': 'When dealing with compound units like kilograms and grams together, precision in place value is essential. A common practical task is converting a mixed measurement such as three kilograms five hundred grams into total grams. We know that three kilograms equals three times one thousand, which is three thousand grams. Adding the five hundred grams yields three thousand five hundred grams. But beware of the famous place-value trap! Consider four kilograms seventy-five grams. A student might hastily write four thousand seven hundred and fifty grams. That is incorrect! Seventy-five grams has zero hundreds. Because one kilogram contains one thousand grams, the gram portion must always occupy three full decimal places. Therefore, four kilograms seventy-five grams is written as four thousand and seventy-five grams, with a zero in the hundreds column. Conversely, when converting a large gram number back into kilograms and grams, the thousands digit represents whole kilograms, while the remaining three digits represent grams. For example, six thousand and eighty grams splits into six kilograms and eighty grams. Eight thousand and five grams splits into eight kilograms and five grams. Remembering the three-digit gram placeholder rule guarantees that you will never misplace a zero during conversion.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🔄</span> Converting Compound Units to Total Grams\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Multiply the kilogram number by 1,000 and add the gram number. Always pad grams with leading zeros if fewer than 3 digits:\n                </p>\n                <div class="space-y-2 text-xs">\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60">\n                        <div class="flex justify-between font-bold text-slate-200">\n                            <span>3 kg 500 g</span>\n                            <span class="text-emerald-400">3,500 g</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-1">3 × 1,000 g + 500 g = 3,000 g + 500 g</div>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60">\n                        <div class="flex justify-between font-bold text-slate-200">\n                            <span>5 kg 40 g</span>\n                            <span class="text-emerald-400">5,040 g <span class="text-amber-400 text-[10px]">(not 5,400 g!)</span></span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-1">5 × 1,000 g + 040 g = 5,000 g + 40 g</div>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60">\n                        <div class="flex justify-between font-bold text-slate-200">\n                            <span>7 kg 6 g</span>\n                            <span class="text-emerald-400">7,006 g <span class="text-amber-400 text-[10px]">(not 7,600 g!)</span></span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-1">7 × 1,000 g + 006 g = 7,000 g + 6 g</div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>🔍</span> Converting Grams to Kilograms & Grams\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                        Split the number at the thousands place: the thousands part gives whole kilograms, and the last 3 digits give remaining grams.\n                    </p>\n                    <div class="space-y-2 text-xs">\n                        <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                            <span class="font-mono text-cyan-300 font-bold">4,825 g</span>\n                            <span class="text-slate-400">➔</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-slate-200">4 kg 825 g</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                            <span class="font-mono text-cyan-300 font-bold">9,070 g</span>\n                            <span class="text-slate-400">➔</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-slate-200">9 kg 70 g</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                            <span class="font-mono text-cyan-300 font-bold">6,008 g</span>\n                            <span class="text-slate-400">➔</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-slate-200">6 kg 8 g</span>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-800/40 text-[11px] text-amber-200 mt-3">\n                    ⚠️ <strong>Trap Alert:</strong> 4 kg 50 g is NOT 4,500 g! 4,500 g is 4 kg 500 g. 50 grams must be written as 050, yielding 4,050 g.\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c3_1', 'question': 'Anika buys a watermelon weighing 4 kg 65 g. What is the total mass of the watermelon in grams?', 'options': ['4,650 g', '4,065 g', '465 g', '40,065 g'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': '4 kg = 4,000 g. Add 65 g: 4,000 + 65.', 'explanation': '4 kg = 4,000 g. Adding 65 g gives 4,000 + 65 = 4,065 g. 65 grams requires a leading zero in the hundreds place.'}, {'id': 'c3_2', 'question': 'A parcel at the post office weighs exactly 8,040 grams. Express this weight in kilograms and grams.', 'options': ['80 kg 40 g', '8 kg 400 g', '8 kg 40 g', '80 kg 4 g'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': 'Divide by 1,000. The quotient is kilograms, and the remainder is grams.', 'explanation': '8,040 ÷ 1,000 = 8 with a remainder of 40. Therefore, 8,040 g = 8 kg 40 g.'}]}, {'id': 4, 'title': 'Comparing Weights', 'tagline': 'Ordering & Inequality • Picnic Fruit Basket Analysis & Relational Signs', 'lecture': 'When comparing different masses, especially when quantities are expressed in mixed units, we must adopt a structured comparison strategy. Imagine a school picnic basket containing four fresh fruits: a heavy watermelon weighing three kilograms two hundred grams, a pineapple weighing one kilogram eight hundred grams, a bag of crisp apples weighing two kilograms five hundred grams, and a box of ripe mangoes weighing one kilogram two hundred and fifty grams. Which fruit is the heaviest, and which is the lightest? The most reliable mathematical method is to convert every single quantity into a single common unit—grams. Let us convert: the watermelon is three thousand two hundred grams, the pineapple is one thousand eight hundred grams, the apples are two thousand five hundred grams, and the mangoes are one thousand two hundred and fifty grams. Now, comparing these four whole numbers is straightforward! Three thousand two hundred is the greatest, so the watermelon is the heaviest. One thousand two hundred and fifty is the smallest, so the mangoes are the lightest. Arranging them in descending order gives: watermelon greater than apples, greater than pineapple, greater than mangoes. In symbols: 3,200 g > 2,500 g > 1,800 g > 1,250 g. Always convert to identical units before applying inequality signs.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🧺</span> The Picnic Fruit Basket\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Compare the four fruits by converting each to grams before sorting:\n                </p>\n                <div class="space-y-2 text-xs">\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-200 font-medium">🍉 Watermelon</span>\n                        <span class="text-slate-400">3 kg 200 g</span>\n                        <span class="font-mono font-bold text-emerald-400">3,200 g (Rank 1 - Heaviest)</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-200 font-medium">🍎 Apples</span>\n                        <span class="text-slate-400">2 kg 500 g</span>\n                        <span class="font-mono font-bold text-cyan-400">2,500 g (Rank 2)</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-200 font-medium">🍍 Pineapple</span>\n                        <span class="text-slate-400">1 kg 800 g</span>\n                        <span class="font-mono font-bold text-amber-400">1,800 g (Rank 3)</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-200 font-medium">🥭 Mangoes</span>\n                        <span class="text-slate-400">1 kg 250 g</span>\n                        <span class="font-mono font-bold text-rose-400">1,250 g (Rank 4 - Lightest)</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>⚖️</span> Inequality Comparisons (<, =, >)\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                        Always evaluate expressions by converting both sides to grams before comparing:\n                    </p>\n                    <div class="space-y-2 text-xs">\n                        <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between font-mono">\n                            <span class="text-slate-200">2 kg 400 g</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-amber-400">></span>\n                            <span class="text-slate-200">2,040 g</span>\n                            <span class="text-[10px] text-slate-400">(2,400 g > 2,040 g)</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between font-mono">\n                            <span class="text-slate-200">5 kg 80 g</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-cyan-400"><</span>\n                            <span class="text-slate-200">5,800 g</span>\n                            <span class="text-[10px] text-slate-400">(5,080 g < 5,800 g)</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between font-mono">\n                            <span class="text-slate-200">3 1/2 kg</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">=</span>\n                            <span class="text-slate-200">3,500 g</span>\n                            <span class="text-[10px] text-slate-400">(3,500 g = 3,500 g)</span>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-800/40 text-[11px] text-emerald-200 mt-3">\n                    💡 <strong>Descending Order Chain:</strong> 3,200 g > 2,500 g > 1,800 g > 1,250 g (Watermelon > Apples > Pineapple > Mangoes).\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c4_1', 'question': 'Which relational symbol correctly completes the mathematical statement: 4 kg 90 g ____ 4,900 g?', 'options': ['> (greater than)', '< (less than)', '= (equal to)', '≈ (approximately equal)'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Convert 4 kg 90 g into grams first.', 'explanation': "4 kg 90 g = 4,000 g + 90 g = 4,090 g. Comparing 4,090 g with 4,900 g gives 4,090 g < 4,900 g. Therefore, '<' is correct."}, {'id': 'c4_2', 'question': 'Four packages weigh: Package A = 2 kg 50 g, Package B = 2,500 g, Package C = 2 kg 450 g, Package D = 2,005 g. Which package is the lightest?', 'options': ['Package A', 'Package B', 'Package C', 'Package D'], 'answer': 3, 'hint': 'Convert all packages to grams: A = 2,050 g, B = 2,500 g, C = 2,450 g, D = 2,005 g.', 'explanation': 'In grams: A = 2,050 g, B = 2,500 g, C = 2,450 g, D = 2,005 g. 2,005 g is the smallest quantity, making Package D the lightest.'}]}, {'id': 5, 'title': 'Milligram Measures', 'tagline': 'Smaller Units of Weight • The 1 g = 1,000 mg Micro-Scale', 'lecture': 'While kilograms and grams are suitable for vegetables, groceries, and luggage, certain tiny objects require a vastly more sensitive unit of measurement: the milligram, abbreviated as mg. The prefix milli means one-thousandth. Therefore, exactly one thousand milligrams make up one single gram, and one milligram is one-thousandth of a gram. Where do we encounter milligrams in everyday life? Look closely at a tiny black worker ant scurrying across a garden path: its total mass is approximately five milligrams! A single grain of fine table salt weighs around one milligram. When you examine a paracetamol or vitamin tablet, the active medicinal ingredient is formulated in milligrams—such as five hundred milligrams of paracetamol, which equals exactly zero point five grams or half a gram. In jewelry, master goldsmiths measure gold and precious gemstones with milligram precision because even a fraction of a gram has significant monetary value. A small sugar sachet provided with tea contains five grams, which is equal to five thousand milligrams. A light gold nose-pin weighing zero point two grams equals two hundred milligrams. Just as one thousand grams make a kilogram, one thousand milligrams make a gram. This metric symmetry makes calculations intuitive and elegant.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🐜</span> Micro-Scale Real-World Benchmarks\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Milligrams measure microscopic and ultra-light masses where grams are too coarse:\n                </p>\n                <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 text-xs">\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">🐜 Worker Ant</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">~5 mg</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">🧂 Grain of Salt</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">~1 mg</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">💊 Paracetamol Pill</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-cyan-400">500 mg (0.5 g)</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">💍 Gold Ring</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-amber-400">4,000 mg (4 g)</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">🪶 Pigeon Feather</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">~15 mg</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">☕ Sugar Sachet</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-cyan-400">5,000 mg (5 g)</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>🔬</span> The Milligram Conversion Rule\n                    </h4>\n                    <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-gradient-to-r from-emerald-500/10 via-cyan-500/10 to-indigo-500/10 border border-emerald-500/30 text-center my-3">\n                        <div class="text-2xl font-black text-emerald-300 tracking-wide">1 g = 1,000 mg</div>\n                        <div class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-1">1 mg = 1/1,000 g = 0.001 g</div>\n                    </div>\n                    <ul class="text-xs text-slate-300 space-y-2 leading-relaxed">\n                        <li class="flex items-start gap-2">\n                            <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">•</span>\n                            <span><strong>Grams to Milligrams:</strong> Multiply grams by 1,000 (e.g., 2.5 g × 1,000 = 2,500 mg).</span>\n                        </li>\n                        <li class="flex items-start gap-2">\n                            <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">•</span>\n                            <span><strong>Milligrams to Grams:</strong> Divide milligrams by 1,000 (e.g., 750 mg ÷ 1,000 = 0.75 g = 3/4 g).</span>\n                        </li>\n                    </ul>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-cyan-950/40 border border-cyan-800/40 text-[11px] text-cyan-200 mt-3">\n                    🏥 <strong>Medical Accuracy:</strong> Pharmaceutical prescriptions are strictly calculated in mg. Misplacing a single zero could deliver ten times the required medicine!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c5_1', 'question': 'A doctor prescribes two 250 mg tablets of calcium twice every day. How many grams of calcium does the patient consume in one full day?', 'options': ['0.5 g', '1 g', '2 g', '1,000 g'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Calculate total milligrams first: 2 tablets × 250 mg = 500 mg per dose. 2 doses per day.', 'explanation': 'Each dose is 2 × 250 mg = 500 mg. With 2 doses per day, the daily total is 500 mg × 2 = 1,000 mg. Since 1,000 mg = 1 g, the patient takes exactly 1 g of calcium daily.'}, {'id': 'c5_2', 'question': 'A goldsmith melts three small gold flakes weighing 350 mg, 450 mg, and 200 mg together. What is the total mass of the melted gold in grams?', 'options': ['1 g', '10 g', '0.1 g', '100 g'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Add the milligrams together: 350 + 450 + 200 mg.', 'explanation': '350 mg + 450 mg + 200 mg = 1,000 mg. Since 1,000 mg = 1 g, the melted gold weighs exactly 1 g.'}]}, {'id': 6, 'title': 'Heavy Metric Units', 'tagline': 'Did You Know? • Quintals, Metric Tonnes & The Elephant Boat Puzzle', 'lecture': "When agricultural grain harvests or massive animals like elephants and blue whales are weighed, kilograms become too small and cumbersome. To handle these heavy masses, commercial commerce and logistics introduce two larger metric units: the quintal and the metric tonne. One quintal equals exactly one hundred kilograms. A mandi wholesale farmer measures wheat, rice, and mustard harvests in quintals. Ten quintals equal one thousand kilograms, which defines exactly one metric tonne! In international shipping and transport, cargo containers and freight trains are rated in tonnes. An adult Asian elephant weighs approximately four metric tonnes, which equals four thousand kilograms or forty quintals! The blue whale, the largest animal on Earth, can reach an astonishing one hundred and fifty tonnes—that is one hundred and fifty thousand kilograms! How did ancient people weigh massive creatures without gigantic modern scales? An ingenious legend tells of an Indian king who vowed to donate gold equal to an elephant's weight. A wise minister loaded the royal elephant onto a large boat and painted a line along the water level on the hull. When the elephant stepped off, the boat floated higher. The minister then filled the boat with heavy stones until the water reached the painted line again, and weighed the stones individually on small balances. This brilliant Archimedean displacement solved the challenge!", 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🚜</span> The Heavy Metric Hierarchy\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Notice how base-100 and base-1,000 relationships connect kilograms to quintals and tonnes:\n                </p>\n                <div class="space-y-2 text-xs">\n                    <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60">\n                        <div class="flex justify-between font-bold text-amber-300 text-sm">\n                            <span>1 Quintal (q)</span>\n                            <span class="text-emerald-400">= 100 kg</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-1">Used for mandi wholesale grain trading: wheat, mustard, cotton, onions.</p>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60">\n                        <div class="flex justify-between font-bold text-cyan-300 text-sm">\n                            <span>1 Metric Tonne (t)</span>\n                            <span class="text-emerald-400">= 10 Quintals = 1,000 kg</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-1">Used for cargo trucks, steel girders, ships, airplanes, and heavy machinery.</p>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60">\n                        <div class="flex justify-between font-bold text-indigo-300 text-sm">\n                            <span>🐘 Asian Elephant</span>\n                            <span class="text-emerald-400">~4 Tonnes (4,000 kg = 40 q)</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-1">A blue whale weighs ~150 tonnes = 150,000 kg = 1,500 quintals!</p>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>🚢</span> The King\'s Elephant Boat Puzzle\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">\n                        Ancient Indian engineers utilized water buoyancy to weigh massive creatures without large scales:\n                    </p>\n                    <ol class="text-xs text-slate-300 space-y-1.5 list-decimal list-inside leading-relaxed">\n                        <li><strong>Step 1:</strong> Walk the elephant onto a large flat-bottomed boat in a calm river.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Step 2:</strong> Mark the exact waterline on the wooden hull with chalk or paint.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Step 3:</strong> Lead the elephant off; the boat rises as displaced water drops.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Step 4:</strong> Fill the boat with stones until it sinks back exactly to the chalk line.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Step 5:</strong> Weigh the stones in small 50 kg sacks using standard balances!</li>\n                    </ol>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-800/40 text-[11px] text-amber-200 mt-3">\n                    💡 <strong>Physical Law:</strong> A floating boat displaces an amount of water whose weight exactly equals the total mass placed inside the boat!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c6_1', 'question': 'A farmer harvests 35 quintals of wheat from his field. How many metric tonnes and kilograms is this harvest equivalent to?', 'options': ['3.5 tonnes (3,500 kg)', '35 tonnes (35,000 kg)', '0.35 tonnes (350 kg)', '350 tonnes (3,500 kg)'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '1 quintal = 100 kg. Multiply 35 by 100 to get kg, then divide by 1,000 to get tonnes.', 'explanation': '35 quintals × 100 kg/quintal = 3,500 kg. Since 1,000 kg = 1 tonne, 3,500 kg ÷ 1,000 = 3.5 metric tonnes.'}, {'id': 'c6_2', 'question': 'A transport truck is permitted to carry a maximum payload of 8 metric tonnes. If it is already loaded with 55 quintals of cement, how many more kilograms of cargo can it carry?', 'options': ['2,500 kg', '3,500 kg', '5,500 kg', '1,500 kg'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Convert everything to kilograms: 8 tonnes = 8,000 kg, 55 quintals = 5,500 kg.', 'explanation': 'Maximum limit = 8 tonnes = 8,000 kg. Current load = 55 quintals = 5,500 kg. Remaining capacity = 8,000 kg - 5,500 kg = 2,500 kg (or 25 quintals / 2.5 tonnes).'}]}, {'id': 7, 'title': 'Adding Weights', 'tagline': 'The Grocery Store • Column Addition & Base-1,000 Regrouping', 'lecture': 'When shopping for family festivals or managing restaurant inventory, we often need to add multiple quantities of mixed weights together. The golden rule of adding compound units is to keep kilograms and grams in distinct, well-aligned vertical columns, just like place values in standard addition. Always start your addition from the rightmost column—the grams. If the sum of the grams column reaches or exceeds one thousand grams, regrouping is required because one thousand grams equals one whole kilogram! For instance, consider a wedding caterer who purchases forty-five kilograms seven hundred and fifty grams of basmati rice and thirty-eight kilograms six hundred and fifty grams of whole wheat flour. Setting them in columns: first, add the grams: seven hundred and fifty plus six hundred and fifty equals one thousand four hundred grams. We decompose one thousand four hundred grams into one thousand grams plus four hundred grams. The one thousand grams converts into one kilogram and is carried over to the kilograms column, leaving four hundred grams written in the grams total. Next, add the kilograms column: forty-five plus thirty-eight plus the one carried-over kilogram equals eighty-four kilograms. The total combined mass is eighty-four kilograms four hundred grams. Master column alignment and carry-over to add weights with complete confidence.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🛒</span> Wedding Caterer Grocery Addition\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Study the vertical column algorithm. Notice how 1,400 g splits into 1 kg carry and 400 g remainder:\n                </p>\n                <div class="p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 font-mono text-xs">\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-400 border-b border-slate-700 pb-1 mb-2">\n                        <span>ITEM</span>\n                        <span class="w-16 text-right">KG</span>\n                        <span class="w-20 text-right">GRAMS</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-amber-400 text-[11px] mb-1">\n                        <span>Regrouping Carry:</span>\n                        <span class="w-16 text-right font-bold">(+1)</span>\n                        <span class="w-20 text-right">-</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-200 py-1">\n                        <span>Basmati Rice:</span>\n                        <span class="w-16 text-right font-bold">45</span>\n                        <span class="w-20 text-right font-bold">750</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-200 py-1 border-b border-slate-700">\n                        <span>Wheat Flour:</span>\n                        <span class="w-16 text-right font-bold">38</span>\n                        <span class="w-20 text-right font-bold">650</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-emerald-400 font-black text-sm pt-2">\n                        <span>TOTAL:</span>\n                        <span class="w-16 text-right">84 kg</span>\n                        <span class="w-20 text-right">400 g</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>⚙️</span> The 3-Step Column Addition Algorithm\n                    </h4>\n                    <ol class="text-xs text-slate-300 space-y-2 list-decimal list-inside leading-relaxed">\n                        <li>\n                            <strong>Column Alignment:</strong> Separate kilograms into the left column and grams into the right column. Ensure grams have 3-digit representation.\n                        </li>\n                        <li>\n                            <strong>Sum Grams First:</strong> Add the grams column. If the total is less than 1,000, write it down directly.\n                        </li>\n                        <li>\n                            <strong>Base-1,000 Regrouping:</strong> If grams ≥ 1,000, divide by 1,000. Carry the whole quotient to the kg column and write the remainder in the grams column.\n                        </li>\n                        <li>\n                            <strong>Sum Kilograms:</strong> Add all numbers in the kilograms column, including any carry-over.\n                        </li>\n                    </ol>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-indigo-950/40 border border-indigo-800/40 text-[11px] text-indigo-200 mt-3">\n                    💡 <strong>Quick Check:</strong> 750 g + 650 g = 1,400 g = 1 kg 400 g. 45 kg + 38 kg + 1 kg = 84 kg. Final Answer: 84 kg 400 g.\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c7_1', 'question': 'A vegetable seller sells 16 kg 450 g of potatoes in the morning and 23 kg 800 g of potatoes in the evening. What is the total weight of potatoes sold?', 'options': ['39 kg 250 g', '40 kg 250 g', '40 kg 1,250 g', '39 kg 1,250 g'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Add the grams first: 450 + 800 = 1,250 g. Regroup 1,000 g as 1 kg.', 'explanation': 'Grams: 450 + 800 = 1,250 g = 1 kg 250 g. Kilograms: 16 + 23 + 1 (carried) = 40 kg. Total = 40 kg 250 g.'}, {'id': 'c7_2', 'question': 'A dry fruit gift box contains 1 kg 250 g of almonds, 850 g of cashews, and 1 kg 400 g of walnuts. What is the total mass of the dry fruits?', 'options': ['3 kg 500 g', '3 kg 50 g', '2 kg 500 g', '3,050 g'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Add all grams together: 1,250 g + 850 g + 1,400 g.', 'explanation': 'Total in grams = 1,250 g + 850 g + 1,400 g = 3,500 g. Converting to compound units gives 3 kg 500 g (or 3 1/2 kg).'}]}, {'id': 8, 'title': 'Subtracting Weights', 'tagline': 'The Grocery Store • Column Subtraction with Base-1,000 Borrowing', 'lecture': 'Subtracting mixed weights requires special care whenever the gram value in the top quantity is smaller than the gram value being subtracted. In ordinary base-ten arithmetic, borrowing brings over ten units from the next column. But in compound weight measurement, borrowing one unit from the kilogram column brings over exactly one thousand grams into the grams column! Let us examine a real-world story from a bustling restaurant. The kitchen starts the week with a fresh sack containing fifty kilograms two hundred grams of red onions. By Friday night, the chef has cooked twenty-seven kilograms six hundred and fifty grams of onions for gravies and curries. How many onions remain? Aligning columns: in the grams column, we must subtract six hundred and fifty grams from two hundred grams. That is impossible without borrowing. We borrow one kilogram from the fifty kilograms, reducing it to forty-nine kilograms. That one borrowed kilogram transforms into one thousand grams, which we immediately add to the existing two hundred grams, giving one thousand two hundred grams. Now, subtract the grams: one thousand two hundred minus six hundred and fifty equals five hundred and fifty grams. Next, subtract the kilograms: forty-nine minus twenty-seven equals twenty-two kilograms. Exactly twenty-two kilograms five hundred and fifty grams of onions remain in the kitchen.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🧅</span> Restaurant Onion Inventory Subtraction\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Watch how 1 kg is borrowed and converted into +1,000 g in the grams column:\n                </p>\n                <div class="p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 font-mono text-xs">\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-400 border-b border-slate-700 pb-1 mb-2">\n                        <span>STAGE</span>\n                        <span class="w-16 text-right">KG</span>\n                        <span class="w-20 text-right">GRAMS</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-amber-400 text-[11px] mb-1">\n                        <span>After Borrowing 1 kg:</span>\n                        <span class="w-16 text-right font-bold">49</span>\n                        <span class="w-20 text-right font-bold">(1,200)</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-200 py-1">\n                        <span>Original Stock:</span>\n                        <span class="w-16 text-right font-bold">50</span>\n                        <span class="w-20 text-right font-bold">200</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-200 py-1 border-b border-slate-700">\n                        <span>Consumed Onions:</span>\n                        <span class="w-16 text-right font-bold">- 27</span>\n                        <span class="w-20 text-right font-bold">- 650</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-emerald-400 font-black text-sm pt-2">\n                        <span>REMAINING:</span>\n                        <span class="w-16 text-right">22 kg</span>\n                        <span class="w-20 text-right">550 g</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>⚠️</span> The Base-1,000 Borrowing Trap\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">\n                        Never borrow "10" or "100" from kilograms! Because 1 kg = 1,000 g, every borrowed kilogram adds exactly 1,000 to the existing grams:\n                    </p>\n                    <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-rose-950/40 border border-rose-800/40 text-xs text-rose-200 mb-2">\n                        <span class="font-bold text-rose-300">❌ Common Mistake:</span> Turning 200 g into 300 g or 120 g by borrowing 1 or 10.\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-800/40 text-xs text-emerald-200">\n                        <span class="font-bold text-emerald-300">✓ Correct Rule:</span> Borrowed 1 kg = +1,000 g. So 200 g + 1,000 g = 1,200 g. Then 1,200 g - 650 g = 550 g.\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-cyan-950/40 border border-cyan-800/40 text-[11px] text-cyan-200 mt-3">\n                    🔍 <strong>Verification by Addition:</strong> Check your answer by adding: 22 kg 550 g + 27 kg 650 g = 50 kg 200 g. It matches perfectly!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c8_1', 'question': 'A sandbag originally weighed 15 kg 100 g. Due to a small tear, 3 kg 750 g of sand leaked out. What is the remaining mass of the sandbag?', 'options': ['12 kg 350 g', '11 kg 350 g', '11 kg 650 g', '12 kg 650 g'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Borrow 1 kg (1,000 g) from 15 kg to make 1,100 g. Then subtract 750 g.', 'explanation': 'Borrow 1 kg from 15 kg, leaving 14 kg. Grams become 100 + 1,000 = 1,100 g. Grams: 1,100 - 750 = 350 g. Kilograms: 14 - 3 = 11 kg. Remaining sand = 11 kg 350 g.'}, {'id': 'c8_2', 'question': 'A community kitchen has a container with 10 kg of sugar. After baking halwa, 4 kg 325 g of sugar is left. How much sugar was used in cooking?', 'options': ['5 kg 675 g', '6 kg 675 g', '5 kg 325 g', '6 kg 775 g'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Write 10 kg as 10 kg 000 g. Borrow 1 kg to make 9 kg 1,000 g.', 'explanation': '10 kg 000 g - 4 kg 325 g: Borrow 1 kg from 10 kg to get 9 kg 1,000 g. Grams: 1,000 - 325 = 675 g. Kilograms: 9 - 4 = 5 kg. Used sugar = 5 kg 675 g.'}]}, {'id': 9, 'title': 'Multiplying Weights', 'tagline': 'More Operations on Weight • Scaling Quantities & Double Number Lines', 'lecture': 'When dealing with multiple identical packages or scaling up a culinary recipe, we use multiplication. There are two robust mathematical pathways to multiply compound weights by a whole number. Let us examine both methods by finding the total mass of four identical sacks of potatoes, where each sack weighs two kilograms three hundred and fifty grams. In Method One, we multiply the units separately: four times two kilograms equals eight kilograms, and four times three hundred and fifty grams equals one thousand four hundred grams. Since one thousand four hundred grams exceeds one thousand, we regroup it into one kilogram and four hundred grams. Adding this carried kilogram to the eight kilograms gives nine kilograms four hundred grams. In Method Two, we convert the entire compound weight into total grams first: two kilograms three hundred and fifty grams becomes two thousand three hundred and fifty grams. Multiplying two thousand three hundred and fifty by four gives nine thousand four hundred grams. Finally, dividing by one thousand returns nine kilograms four hundred grams. Both methods yield the exact same answer! We can also visualize this proportional scaling along a double number line when adjusting recipes—doubling ingredients for four people to feed eight people. Both strategies provide clear mathematical pathways.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🥔</span> Two Methods to Multiply Mixed Weights\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Calculate: 4 sacks × 2 kg 350 g per sack:\n                </p>\n                <div class="space-y-2 text-xs">\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60">\n                        <div class="font-bold text-cyan-300 mb-1">Method 1: Unit-by-Unit Multiplication</div>\n                        <ul class="text-slate-300 space-y-1 text-[11px]">\n                            <li>• Multiply kg: 4 × 2 kg = 8 kg</li>\n                            <li>• Multiply g: 4 × 350 g = 1,400 g = 1 kg 400 g</li>\n                            <li>• Combine: 8 kg + 1 kg 400 g = <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">9 kg 400 g</span></li>\n                        </ul>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60">\n                        <div class="font-bold text-amber-300 mb-1">Method 2: Convert to Grams First</div>\n                        <ul class="text-slate-300 space-y-1 text-[11px]">\n                            <li>• Convert: 2 kg 350 g = 2,350 g</li>\n                            <li>• Multiply: 2,350 g × 4 = 9,400 g</li>\n                            <li>• Reconvert: 9,400 g = <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">9 kg 400 g</span></li>\n                        </ul>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>👩\u200d🍳</span> Recipe Proportional Scaling\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">\n                        When scaling up food preparation, multiply all ingredient quantities by the same scaling factor:\n                    </p>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 space-y-1.5 text-xs">\n                        <div class="flex justify-between border-b border-slate-700 pb-1 font-semibold text-slate-300">\n                            <span>Biryani Ingredient</span>\n                            <span>For 4 People</span>\n                            <span class="text-emerald-400">For 8 People (×2)</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>Basmati Rice</span>\n                            <span>500 g</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">1,000 g (1 kg)</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>Vegetables</span>\n                            <span>750 g</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">1,500 g (1.5 kg)</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>Ghee</span>\n                            <span>125 g</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">250 g (1/4 kg)</span>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-indigo-950/40 border border-indigo-800/40 text-[11px] text-indigo-200 mt-3">\n                    💡 <strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Converting to total grams first is completely immune to carry-over confusion during multi-digit multiplication!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c9_1', 'question': 'A baker prepares 6 fruit cakes. Each cake requires 1 kg 250 g of mixed candied fruit. What is the total weight of candied fruit needed?', 'options': ['6 kg 500 g', '7 kg 500 g', '7 kg 250 g', '6 kg 250 g'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Convert to grams: 1,250 g × 6, or multiply 1 kg × 6 and 250 g × 6.', 'explanation': 'Method 1: 6 × 1 kg = 6 kg, and 6 × 250 g = 1,500 g = 1 kg 500 g. Total = 6 kg + 1 kg 500 g = 7 kg 500 g (or 7 1/2 kg).'}, {'id': 'c9_2', 'question': 'A recipe uses 350 g of paneer to feed 3 family members. How much paneer is required to prepare the same dish for 9 family members?', 'options': ['700 g', '1 kg 50 g', '1 kg 500 g', '1,050 g'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': '9 members is 3 times 3 members. Multiply 350 g by 3.', 'explanation': 'The scaling factor is 9 ÷ 3 = 3. Multiplying 350 g × 3 gives 1,050 g, which converts to 1 kg 50 g.'}]}, {'id': 10, 'title': 'Dividing Weights', 'tagline': 'More Operations on Weight • Equal Distribution & Scrap Dealer Rates', 'lecture': 'Dividing weight quantities arises when sharing bulk supplies equally among several people or calculating the cost per kilogram at a scrap dealer. Suppose a cooperative store receives a large crate containing seven kilograms five hundred grams of roasted cashew nuts, which must be divided equally into six identical gift packets. How much does each packet weigh? The cleanest strategy is to convert the compound mass into total grams first. Seven kilograms five hundred grams equals seven thousand five hundred grams. Now, perform single whole-number division: seven thousand five hundred divided by six. Seven divided by six gives one with a remainder of one. Bring down five to make fifteen; fifteen divided by six gives two with a remainder of three. Bring down zero to make thirty; thirty divided by six gives five with zero remainder. Finally, bring down the last zero to get zero. The quotient is exactly one thousand two hundred and fifty grams. Splitting this back into compound units gives one kilogram two hundred and fifty grams per packet. We also encounter division when dealing with scrap dealers: if eight kilograms of discarded newspaper fetches one hundred and twelve rupees, dividing one hundred and twelve by eight gives the unit rate of fourteen rupees per kilogram. Division helps us allocate resources fairly and evaluate economic unit values.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🥜</span> Fair Sharing: 7 kg 500 g ÷ 6 Packets\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Converting compound weight to grams simplifies long division into standard arithmetic:\n                </p>\n                <div class="p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 font-mono text-xs space-y-2">\n                    <div class="flex justify-between border-b border-slate-700 pb-1">\n                        <span class="text-slate-400">Total Cashews:</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">7 kg 500 g = 7,500 g</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between border-b border-slate-700 pb-1">\n                        <span class="text-slate-400">Division:</span>\n                        <span class="text-amber-300 font-bold">7,500 g ÷ 6 = 1,250 g</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between pt-1">\n                        <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">Per Packet:</span>\n                        <span class="text-emerald-400 font-black">1 kg 250 g (1 1/4 kg)</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>📰</span> Scrap Dealer Unit Pricing Ledger\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">\n                        Calculate the rate per kg: Rate = Total Earnings ÷ Total Mass:\n                    </p>\n                    <div class="space-y-1.5 text-xs">\n                        <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                            <span class="text-slate-300">🗞️ Old Newspapers</span>\n                            <span class="text-slate-400">8 kg sold for ₹112</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">₹14 / kg</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                            <span class="text-slate-300">🔩 Waste Iron</span>\n                            <span class="text-slate-400">5 kg sold for ₹140</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-cyan-400">₹28 / kg</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                            <span class="text-slate-300">🥤 Plastic Bottles</span>\n                            <span class="text-slate-400">12 kg sold for ₹144</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-amber-400">₹12 / kg</span>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-800/40 text-[11px] text-emerald-200 mt-3">\n                    💡 <strong>Economic Insight:</strong> Knowing the rate per kilogram allows you to predict earnings for any quantity: 20 kg of iron = 20 × ₹28 = ₹560!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c10_1', 'question': 'A 9 kg sack of basmati rice is divided equally into 8 smaller cloth bags. What is the mass of rice in each bag?', 'options': ['1 kg 125 g', '1 kg 250 g', '1 kg 150 g', '1 kg 75 g'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Convert 9 kg to 9,000 g and divide by 8.', 'explanation': '9 kg = 9,000 g. 9,000 ÷ 8 = 1,125 g. Converting back gives 1 kg 125 g (or 1 1/8 kg).'}, {'id': 'c10_2', 'question': 'Rohan sells 15 kg of cardboard boxes to a kabadiwala and receives ₹180 in total. What was the rate per kilogram paid by the kabadiwala?', 'options': ['₹10 per kg', '₹12 per kg', '₹14 per kg', '₹15 per kg'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Divide the total money by the total weight: ₹180 ÷ 15 kg.', 'explanation': 'Rate per kg = Total Price ÷ Total Weight = ₹180 ÷ 15 = ₹12 per kg.'}]}, {'id': 11, 'title': 'Measuring Capacity', 'tagline': 'Introduction to Litres and Millilitres • Everyday Liquid Volumes', 'lecture': "Just as weight measures the gravitational pull on an object's mass, capacity measures the maximum volume of liquid that a three-dimensional hollow container can hold. In the international metric system, the standard unit of capacity is the litre, symbolized by a capital L or lowercase l. For smaller liquid quantities, we use the millilitre, abbreviated as mL. Exactly one thousand millilitres make up one litre. To build an intuitive grasp of capacity, let us explore familiar liquid benchmarks. A small medicinal spoon or dropper holds about five millilitres of syrup. A ceramic teacup holds approximately one hundred and fifty millilitres of tea. A standard glass of drinking water contains about two hundred and fifty millilitres. A tetra-pack juice carton holds one litre, which is one thousand millilitres. In household chores, a bathroom bucket typically holds fifteen to twenty litres of water. The overhead water storage tank installed on a residential rooftop holds between five hundred and one thousand litres! When choosing units, we measure cough syrup, eye drops, ink bottles, and perfumes in millilitres, while water tanks, petrol fuel, milk deliveries, and swimming pools are measured in litres. Recognizing these real-world benchmarks helps us select the correct metric unit for any liquid volume.", 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>💧</span> Real-World Liquid Benchmarks\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Capacity reflects container volume. Notice how units scale across everyday liquids:\n                </p>\n                <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 text-xs">\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">🥄 Medicine Spoon</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">5 mL</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">☕ Chai Cup</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">150 mL</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">🥛 Drinking Glass</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-cyan-400">250 mL</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">🧃 Juice Carton</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-cyan-400">1,000 mL (1 L)</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">🪣 Bath Bucket</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-amber-400">15 to 20 L</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">🏠 Rooftop Tank</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-indigo-400">500 to 1,000 L</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>🧪</span> The Litre-Millilitre Relationship\n                    </h4>\n                    <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-gradient-to-r from-blue-500/10 via-cyan-500/10 to-teal-500/10 border border-cyan-500/30 text-center my-3">\n                        <div class="text-2xl font-black text-cyan-300 tracking-wide">1 Litre (L) = 1,000 mL</div>\n                        <div class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-1">1 mL = 1/1,000 L = 0.001 L</div>\n                    </div>\n                    <ul class="text-xs text-slate-300 space-y-2 leading-relaxed">\n                        <li class="flex items-start gap-2">\n                            <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">•</span>\n                            <span><strong>Quarter Litre (1/4 L):</strong> Exactly 250 mL (one standard drinking glass).</span>\n                        </li>\n                        <li class="flex items-start gap-2">\n                            <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">•</span>\n                            <span><strong>Half Litre (1/2 L):</strong> Exactly 500 mL (two standard drinking glasses).</span>\n                        </li>\n                        <li class="flex items-start gap-2">\n                            <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">•</span>\n                            <span><strong>Three-Quarter Litre (3/4 L):</strong> Exactly 750 mL (three standard drinking glasses).</span>\n                        </li>\n                    </ul>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-cyan-950/40 border border-cyan-800/40 text-[11px] text-cyan-200 mt-3">\n                    💡 <strong>Unit Selection Rule:</strong> If the volume can easily fit inside a small handheld cup, choose mL. If it fills a bucket or tank, choose L.\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c11_1', 'question': 'Which of the following items is most appropriately measured using millilitres (mL) rather than litres (L)?', 'options': ['Water inside a residential rooftop storage tank', "A doctor's eye-drop bottle", 'Petrol pumped into a highway tour bus', 'Water inside an Olympic swimming pool'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Think about which item holds only a few drops or a tiny fraction of a glass.', 'explanation': 'An eye-drop bottle holds around 5 to 10 mL of liquid. Rooftop tanks, bus petrol tanks, and swimming pools hold hundreds to thousands of litres.'}, {'id': 'c11_2', 'question': 'How many 250 mL glasses of water are required to completely fill a 2-litre water jug?', 'options': ['4 glasses', '6 glasses', '8 glasses', '10 glasses'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': 'Convert 2 litres to millilitres: 2 L = 2,000 mL. Then divide by 250 mL.', 'explanation': '2 L = 2,000 mL. Since each glass is 250 mL, 2,000 ÷ 250 = 8 glasses (4 glasses make 1 L, so 8 glasses make 2 L).'}]}, {'id': 12, 'title': 'Volume Scales', 'tagline': 'Big to Small, Small to Big • Reading Graduated Cylinders & The Meniscus', 'lecture': 'To measure liquid capacity accurately in science laboratories and pharmacies, we use transparent graduated cylinders, beakers, and measuring jars. These cylinders feature engraved vertical scale markings called graduations. When reading any graduated cylinder, two essential mathematical rules must be observed. First, determine the value of each subdivision mark by checking the interval between major numbered lines. For example, if the distance between zero and one hundred millilitres is divided into ten equal tick spaces, each single subdivision mark represents ten millilitres. If divided into five tick spaces, each mark represents twenty millilitres. Second, observe the unique physical shape of the water surface. Due to surface tension and adhesion with the cylinder wall, water curves slightly upward at the edges, forming a curved dip called a meniscus. The universal scientific convention is to always read the volume at the absolute bottom of this curved meniscus, keeping your eye at the exact horizontal level of the liquid surface. Looking from above causes parallax errors that make the volume seem too high, while looking from below makes it seem too low. Graduated cylinders also show how small units combine to fill larger vessels—such as ten two-hundred-millilitre measuring cups perfectly filling a two-litre jar. Accurate observation ensures scientific precision.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>👁️</span> The Meniscus Eye-Level Reading Rule\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Liquids adhere to glass surfaces, creating a concave curved boundary. Read strictly at the lowest central point:\n                </p>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 space-y-2 text-xs">\n                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 p-2 rounded-lg bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-800/30">\n                        <span class="text-rose-400 font-bold">❌ High Angle:</span>\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">Looking down produces an over-estimation reading error.</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 p-2 rounded-lg bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-800/40">\n                        <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">✓ Eye Level:</span>\n                        <span class="text-slate-200 font-semibold">Align eye directly horizontal with the bottom of the curved meniscus!</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 p-2 rounded-lg bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-800/30">\n                        <span class="text-rose-400 font-bold">❌ Low Angle:</span>\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">Looking up produces an under-estimation reading error.</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>🧪</span> Container Refill Partitions\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">\n                        How many smaller calibrated scoops or cups fill standard bottles?\n                    </p>\n                    <div class="space-y-1.5 text-xs">\n                        <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                            <span class="text-slate-300">1 L Bottle</span>\n                            <span class="text-cyan-300 font-mono">1,000 mL ÷ 100 mL</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">= 10 scoops of 100 mL</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                            <span class="text-slate-300">1 L Bottle</span>\n                            <span class="text-cyan-300 font-mono">1,000 mL ÷ 200 mL</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">= 5 cups of 200 mL</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                            <span class="text-slate-300">2 L Jug</span>\n                            <span class="text-cyan-300 font-mono">2,000 mL ÷ 250 mL</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">= 8 glasses of 250 mL</span>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-800/40 text-[11px] text-amber-200 mt-3">\n                    📏 <strong>Tick Scale Formula:</strong> Value of 1 division = (Upper Mark - Lower Mark) ÷ Number of subdivision spaces.\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c12_1', 'question': 'A 500 mL measuring cylinder has major markings at every 100 mL. Between 200 mL and 300 mL, there are 5 equal spaces. If the liquid meniscus touches the 3rd mark past 200 mL, what is the volume?', 'options': ['230 mL', '260 mL', '215 mL', '250 mL'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Each subdivision space = (300 - 200) ÷ 5 = 100 ÷ 5 = 20 mL.', 'explanation': 'Each division represents 100 ÷ 5 = 20 mL. 3 marks past 200 mL equals 200 + (3 × 20) = 200 + 60 = 260 mL.'}, {'id': 'c12_2', 'question': 'A laboratory technician needs 1 litre 500 mL of saline solution. How many 150 mL test beakers must be poured to obtain this exact volume?', 'options': ['8 beakers', '10 beakers', '12 beakers', '15 beakers'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Convert 1 L 500 mL to mL: 1,500 mL. Divide by 150 mL.', 'explanation': '1 L 500 mL = 1,500 mL. Dividing 1,500 mL ÷ 150 mL = 10 beakers.'}]}, {'id': 13, 'title': 'Capacity Equivalence', 'tagline': "Different Units but Same Measure • Khayal Chacha's Milk Route & 3-Digit mL", 'lecture': "Every morning across towns and villages in India, milk vendors like Khayal chacha deliver fresh milk to neighborhood doorsteps. Khayal chacha carries large steel milk cans on his bicycle, fitted with brass measuring ladles of five hundred millilitres, two hundred and fifty millilitres, and one hundred millilitres. In his delivery diary, Khayal chacha logs each family's daily order. Some customers ask for compound quantities like one litre five hundred millilitres, while others ask for fifteen hundred millilitres. Are these two quantities identical? Yes, absolutely! One litre equals one thousand millilitres; adding five hundred millilitres yields one thousand five hundred millilitres. Just as with weights, capacity obeys the strict three-digit millilitre rule because one litre contains one thousand millilitres. Consider a customer who orders two litres seventy-five millilitres. A careless apprentice might write two thousand seven hundred and fifty millilitres. But that would deliver almost three litres! Seventy-five millilitres has zero hundreds, so it must be written as zero-seven-five. The correct volume is two thousand and seventy-five millilitres. Similarly, three thousand and forty millilitres splits into three litres and forty millilitres. Khayal chacha verifies his weekly accounts by adding customer logs using double number lines that keep litres and millilitres in synchronized harmony.", 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🥛</span> Khayal Chacha\'s Daily Milk Ledger\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Notice how compound litre-millilitre orders translate directly into total millilitres:\n                </p>\n                <div class="space-y-2 text-xs">\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <div>\n                            <div class="font-bold text-slate-200">Sharma Family: 1 L 500 mL</div>\n                            <div class="text-[11px] text-slate-400">1,000 mL + 500 mL</div>\n                        </div>\n                        <span class="font-mono font-bold text-emerald-400">1,500 mL</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <div>\n                            <div class="font-bold text-slate-200">Verma Family: 2 L 75 mL</div>\n                            <div class="text-[11px] text-slate-400">2,000 mL + 075 mL</div>\n                        </div>\n                        <span class="font-mono font-bold text-emerald-400">2,075 mL <span class="text-amber-400 text-[10px]">(not 2,750!)</span></span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <div>\n                            <div class="font-bold text-slate-200">Patel Family: 3 L 8 mL</div>\n                            <div class="text-[11px] text-slate-400">3,000 mL + 008 mL</div>\n                        </div>\n                        <span class="font-mono font-bold text-emerald-400">3,008 mL <span class="text-amber-400 text-[10px]">(not 3,800!)</span></span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>🔍</span> Decomposing Total Millilitres\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                        The digit in the thousands place gives whole litres; the remaining three digits give millilitres:\n                    </p>\n                    <div class="space-y-2 text-xs font-mono">\n                        <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                            <span class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">4,650 mL</span>\n                            <span class="text-slate-400">➔</span>\n                            <span class="text-slate-200">4 L 650 mL</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                            <span class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">5,040 mL</span>\n                            <span class="text-slate-400">➔</span>\n                            <span class="text-slate-200">5 L 40 mL</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                            <span class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">7,005 mL</span>\n                            <span class="text-slate-400">➔</span>\n                            <span class="text-slate-200">7 L 5 mL</span>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-indigo-950/40 border border-indigo-800/40 text-[11px] text-indigo-200 mt-3">\n                    🥛 <strong>Standard Brass Measures:</strong> Khayal chacha uses 500 mL, 200 mL, and 100 mL ladles. To pour 800 mL, he dips the 500 mL ladle once, 200 mL once, and 100 mL once!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c13_1', 'question': 'A milkman delivers 3 litres 60 millilitres of buffalo milk to a sweet shop. What is this volume in total millilitres?', 'options': ['3,600 mL', '3,060 mL', '360 mL', '30,060 mL'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': '3 L = 3,000 mL. Write 60 mL with a leading zero as 060.', 'explanation': '3 L = 3,000 mL. Adding 60 mL gives 3,000 + 60 = 3,060 mL. 3,600 mL would be 3 L 600 mL.'}, {'id': 'c13_2', 'question': 'A water filter pitcher contains 4,050 mL of purified water. How is this quantity written in litres and millilitres?', 'options': ['40 L 50 mL', '4 L 500 mL', '4 L 50 mL', '40 L 5 mL'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': 'Divide by 1,000: 4,050 = 4,000 + 50.', 'explanation': '4,050 mL = 4,000 mL + 50 mL = 4 L 50 mL.'}]}, {'id': 14, 'title': 'Volume Calculations', 'tagline': 'Let Us Think • Dialogue Errors (1,400 mL vs 1,040 mL) & Daily Hydration', 'lecture': 'Clear communication in mathematics prevents serious real-world misunderstandings. In a famous classroom dialogue, two students debated a bottle label: Arjun stated that one litre forty millilitres equals one thousand four hundred millilitres, while Priya maintained it was one thousand and forty millilitres. Who was correct? Priya was absolutely right! One thousand four hundred millilitres equals one litre four hundred millilitres—four hundred millilitres is nearly half a litre, whereas forty millilitres is barely three tablespoons! Confusing forty with four hundred changes the volume by three hundred and sixty millilitres. We also use capacity calculations to monitor daily personal health through hydration tracking. Medical doctors recommend that a ten-year-old child drink approximately two litres five hundred millilitres of water every day to stay active and healthy. Suppose Aarav drinks two hundred and fifty millilitres before school, five hundred millilitres during morning recess, seven hundred and fifty millilitres in the afternoon, and five hundred millilitres after football practice. Adding these amounts: two hundred and fifty plus five hundred plus seven hundred and fifty plus five hundred equals two thousand millilitres, which is two litres. Subtracting two litres from his goal of two point five litres shows he needs five hundred millilitres more before bedtime. Mathematics keeps our bodies properly hydrated!', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🗣️</span> Dialogue Error Analysis\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Compare Arjun\'s claim versus Priya\'s claim on "1 L 40 mL":\n                </p>\n                <div class="space-y-2 text-xs">\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-rose-950/40 border border-rose-800/40">\n                        <div class="font-bold text-rose-300">❌ Arjun\'s Error: 1 L 40 mL = 1,400 mL</div>\n                        <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-300 mt-1">\n                            Arjun misplaced the zero in the tens place! 1,400 mL = 1 L 400 mL (nearly half a litre more than 40 mL).\n                        </p>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-800/40">\n                        <div class="font-bold text-emerald-300">✓ Priya\'s Truth: 1 L 40 mL = 1,040 mL</div>\n                        <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-300 mt-1">\n                            1 L = 1,000 mL. 40 mL has 0 hundreds, 4 tens, 0 ones: 1,000 + 040 = 1,040 mL.\n                        </p>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>🚰</span> Aarav\'s Daily Hydration Log\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">\n                        Recommended Daily Water Target = <strong>2,500 mL (2 L 500 mL)</strong>:\n                    </p>\n                    <div class="space-y-1.5 text-xs">\n                        <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                            <span class="text-slate-300">🌅 Morning Wakeup</span>\n                            <span class="font-mono text-cyan-300">1 glass</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">250 mL</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                            <span class="text-slate-300">🥪 School Lunch Recess</span>\n                            <span class="font-mono text-cyan-300">2 glasses</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">500 mL</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                            <span class="text-slate-300">🏠 Afternoon Homework</span>\n                            <span class="font-mono text-cyan-300">3 glasses</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">750 mL</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                            <span class="text-slate-300">⚽ Evening Sports</span>\n                            <span class="font-mono text-cyan-300">2 glasses</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">500 mL</span>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-blue-950/40 border border-blue-800/40 text-[11px] text-blue-200 mt-3">\n                    💧 <strong>Current Total:</strong> 2,000 mL (2 L). Remaining to goal: 2,500 - 2,000 = 500 mL (2 more glasses before sleep!).\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c14_1', 'question': 'What is the difference in volume between 1 litre 400 millilitres and 1 litre 40 millilitres?', 'options': ['360 mL', '400 mL', '40 mL', '3,960 mL'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Convert both to millilitres: 1,400 mL and 1,040 mL. Subtract them.', 'explanation': '1 L 400 mL = 1,400 mL. 1 L 40 mL = 1,040 mL. Difference = 1,400 - 1,040 = 360 mL.'}, {'id': 'c14_2', 'question': "Meera's doctor recommends drinking 2 L of fluid daily. She drinks 350 mL of milk, 1 L 200 mL of water, and 250 mL of soup. Has she met her daily requirement?", 'options': ['No, she is short by 200 mL', 'Yes, she drank exactly 2 L', 'No, she is short by 400 mL', 'Yes, she drank 200 mL extra'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Sum all fluids: 350 mL + 1,200 mL + 250 mL = 1,800 mL. Target is 2,000 mL.', 'explanation': 'Total fluids = 350 + 1,200 + 250 = 1,800 mL (1 L 800 mL). Her target is 2 L = 2,000 mL. Shortfall = 2,000 - 1,800 = 200 mL.'}]}, {'id': 15, 'title': 'Comparing Capacities', 'tagline': 'Let Us Compare • Petrol Pump Volume Operations & Fuel Arithmetic', 'lecture': 'A highway petrol pump is a dynamic real-world laboratory of capacity arithmetic. At a petrol pump, fuel dispensers meter liquid petrol and diesel with electronic precision, measuring every millilitre pumped into fuel tanks. Different motor vehicles have vastly different fuel tank capacities depending on their engine power and travel range. A motorcycle or two-wheeler has a fuel tank holding about ten to twelve litres. An auto-rickshaw holds about fifteen litres. A family passenger car holds around forty-five litres. A long-distance interstate passenger bus holds about two hundred litres, while a heavy eighteen-wheel multi-axle freight truck has fuel tanks holding up to three hundred and fifty litres or more! Comparing these capacities using relational inequality symbols: two-wheeler less than car, car less than bus, bus less than freight truck. We also solve refuelling operations: if a car with a forty-five litre tank arrives at the pump with twelve litres eight hundred millilitres of fuel remaining, how much fuel is required to fill the tank completely? We subtract twelve litres eight hundred millilitres from forty-five litres. Regrouping one litre as one thousand millilitres from forty-five gives forty-four litres one thousand millilitres. Subtracting leaves thirty-two litres two hundred millilitres. Fuel arithmetic combines practical economics and volume measurement.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>⛽</span> Vehicle Fuel Tank Capacities\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Notice how fuel tank sizes scale across vehicle categories:\n                </p>\n                <div class="space-y-2 text-xs">\n                    <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">🛵 Two-Wheeler Motorcycle</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">~10 to 12 L</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">🛺 Auto-Rickshaw</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">~15 L</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">🚗 Family Sedan / Car</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-cyan-400">~45 L</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">🚌 Tour Bus</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-amber-400">~200 L</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 flex items-center justify-between">\n                        <span class="text-slate-300">🚛 Multi-Axle Freight Truck</span>\n                        <span class="font-bold text-indigo-400">~350 L</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>🧮</span> Fuel Refill Subtraction Algorithm\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">\n                        Fuel needed to fill a 45 L car tank with 12 L 800 mL already inside:\n                    </p>\n                    <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 font-mono text-xs space-y-1">\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-400 border-b border-slate-700 pb-1">\n                            <span>LINE</span>\n                            <span class="w-16 text-right">LITRES</span>\n                            <span class="w-20 text-right">MILLILITRES</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-amber-400 text-[11px]">\n                            <span>Borrow 1 L:</span>\n                            <span class="w-16 text-right">44</span>\n                            <span class="w-20 text-right">(1,000)</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-200">\n                            <span>Full Tank:</span>\n                            <span class="w-16 text-right">45</span>\n                            <span class="w-20 text-right">000</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-200 border-b border-slate-700">\n                            <span>Current Fuel:</span>\n                            <span class="w-16 text-right">- 12</span>\n                            <span class="w-20 text-right">- 800</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-emerald-400 font-bold text-sm pt-1">\n                            <span>NEEDED:</span>\n                            <span class="w-16 text-right">32 L</span>\n                            <span class="w-20 text-right">200 mL</span>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-800/40 text-[11px] text-emerald-200 mt-3">\n                    💡 <strong>Inequality Chain:</strong> 12 L < 15 L < 45 L < 200 L < 350 L (Motorcycle < Auto < Car < Bus < Truck).\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c15_1', 'question': 'A motorcycle tank has a capacity of 12 litres. If it currently contains 4 litres 350 millilitres of petrol, how much more petrol is required to top up the tank completely?', 'options': ['7 L 650 mL', '8 L 650 mL', '7 L 350 mL', '8 L 350 mL'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Borrow 1 L from 12 L to make 11 L 1,000 mL. Subtract 4 L 350 mL.', 'explanation': '12 L 000 mL - 4 L 350 mL: Borrow 1 L to get 11 L 1,000 mL. Millilitres: 1,000 - 350 = 650 mL. Litres: 11 - 4 = 7 L. Needed = 7 L 650 mL.'}, {'id': 'c15_2', 'question': 'A tour bus driver started the morning with 65 L of diesel. During the trip, 120 L 500 mL of diesel was added. At the end of the trip, 32 L 200 mL remained. How much diesel was consumed?', 'options': ['153 L 300 mL', '152 L 300 mL', '153 L 700 mL', '185 L 500 mL'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Total diesel available = 65 L + 120 L 500 mL = 185 L 500 mL. Subtract the remaining 32 L 200 mL.', 'explanation': 'Total fuel = 65 L 000 mL + 120 L 500 mL = 185 L 500 mL. Consumed fuel = 185 L 500 mL - 32 L 200 mL = 153 L 300 mL.'}]}, {'id': 16, 'title': 'Multi-step Problems', 'tagline': 'Let Us Solve • Juice Vendors, Oil Partitioning & Commercial Packaging', 'lecture': 'In practical commerce and community life, mathematical challenges rarely consist of a single isolated calculation. Instead, they require multi-step reasoning: converting units, executing division or multiplication, and calculating monetary costs or earnings. Consider a sugarcane juice vendor who prepares a large ten-litre insulated dispenser of freshly squeezed cane juice for an afternoon cricket tournament. The vendor serves the juice in uniform paper cups holding two hundred millilitres each, charging fifteen rupees per cup. How many cups can he serve, and how much total money does he earn? Step one: convert ten litres into millilitres: ten times one thousand equals ten thousand millilitres. Step two: divide ten thousand millilitres by two hundred millilitres per cup: ten thousand divided by two hundred equals fifty cups. Step three: multiply the number of cups by the price per cup: fifty cups times fifteen rupees equals seven hundred and fifty rupees total earnings! Another multi-step task involves packaging fifteen litres of mustard oil into seven hundred and fifty millilitre bottles: fifteen thousand divided by seven hundred and fifty equals exactly twenty bottles. Solving multi-step problems systematically by breaking them into logical sequential stages empowers you to master real-world mathematics with complete accuracy.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🥤</span> Sugarcane Juice Vendor Logistics\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Analyze the 3-step breakdown for 10 L of juice sold in 200 mL cups at ₹15 each:\n                </p>\n                <div class="space-y-2 text-xs">\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60">\n                        <div class="flex justify-between font-bold text-slate-200">\n                            <span>Step 1: Unit Harmonization</span>\n                            <span class="text-cyan-400">10,000 mL</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-0.5">10 L × 1,000 mL/L = 10,000 mL total juice.</p>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60">\n                        <div class="flex justify-between font-bold text-slate-200">\n                            <span>Step 2: Container Partitioning</span>\n                            <span class="text-amber-400">50 Cups</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-0.5">10,000 mL ÷ 200 mL per cup = 50 cups served.</p>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60">\n                        <div class="flex justify-between font-bold text-slate-200">\n                            <span>Step 3: Revenue Calculation</span>\n                            <span class="text-emerald-400">₹750 Total</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-0.5">50 cups × ₹15 per cup = ₹750 earned.</p>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>🧴</span> Mustard Oil Partitioning Station\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">\n                        Dividing a 15-litre bulk tin into 750 mL commercial retail bottles:\n                    </p>\n                    <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 font-mono text-xs space-y-1.5">\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>Bulk Oil Tin:</span>\n                            <span class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">15 L = 15,000 mL</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>Bottle Volume:</span>\n                            <span class="text-amber-300 font-bold">750 mL per bottle</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between border-t border-slate-700 pt-1 text-emerald-400 font-black text-sm">\n                            <span>Total Bottles Packed:</span>\n                            <span>15,000 ÷ 750 = 20 Bottles</span>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-indigo-950/40 border border-indigo-800/40 text-[11px] text-indigo-200 mt-3">\n                    📌 <strong>General Strategy:</strong> (1) Convert all quantities to the smaller unit. (2) Divide total volume by unit size. (3) Multiply count by unit price.\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c16_1', 'question': 'A dairy cooperative has 24 litres of buttermilk. They pack it into pouches holding 400 mL each and sell each pouch for ₹12. What is their total revenue if all pouches are sold?', 'options': ['₹600', '₹720', '₹840', '₹960'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Convert 24 L to 24,000 mL. Find number of pouches: 24,000 ÷ 400. Then multiply by ₹12.', 'explanation': '24 L = 24,000 mL. Number of pouches = 24,000 ÷ 400 = 60 pouches. Total revenue = 60 × ₹12 = ₹720.'}, {'id': 'c16_2', 'question': 'A picnic group brings three 2-litre bottles of lemonade. They pour 150 mL into each of 35 paper cups for the children. How much lemonade remains in the bottles?', 'options': ['750 mL', '500 mL', '1 L 250 mL', '1,750 mL'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Total lemonade = 3 × 2,000 mL = 6,000 mL. Used lemonade = 35 × 150 mL = 5,250 mL.', 'explanation': 'Total lemonade = 3 × 2,000 mL = 6,000 mL. Lemonade poured = 35 × 150 mL = 5,250 mL. Remaining = 6,000 - 5,250 = 750 mL (or 3/4 L).'}]}, {'id': 17, 'title': 'Challenge 1', 'tagline': 'Mastery Challenge 1 • Weight Estimation, Scale Readings & Equivalences', 'lecture': 'Welcome to Mastery Challenge 1. In this interactive session, you will test your understanding of real-world mass estimations, reading analog and digital weighing scales, and converting between kilograms and grams.', 'content_html': "<p class='text-xs text-slate-300 mb-4'>Test your mastery of mass estimation, scale reading, and metric conversions. Answer all 10 questions to unlock the detailed solutions!</p>", 'questions': [{'id': 'q1', 'question': 'Which of the following household items would have an estimated mass closest to 30 to 40 kg?', 'options': ['A plastic lunchbox filled with food', 'A standard two-seater living room sofa', 'A steel kitchen fork', 'An empty 1-litre water bottle'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Think about furniture that requires two adults to lift comfortably.', 'explanation': 'A standard two-seater sofa weighs around 35 kg. A lunchbox is ~500 g, a fork is ~40 g, and an empty water bottle is ~50 g.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 text-slate-200'>\n                        <strong>Analysis of Mass Benchmarks:</strong>\n                        <ul class='list-disc list-inside mt-1 space-y-1 text-slate-300'>\n                            <li>Steel kitchen fork: ~30 to 50 g (grams)</li>\n                            <li>Plastic lunchbox with food: ~400 to 600 g</li>\n                            <li>Empty water bottle: ~30 to 50 g</li>\n                            <li>Two-seater sofa: <strong>~30 to 40 kg (kilograms)</strong></li>\n                        </ul>\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: A standard two-seater living room sofa (~35 kg).</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q2', 'question': 'An analog spring scale has a dial where the interval between 4 kg and 5 kg is subdivided into 4 equal spaces. If the dial pointer rests on the 3rd mark past 4 kg, what weight is displayed?', 'options': ['4 kg 300 g', '4 kg 750 g', '4 kg 250 g', '4 kg 500 g'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Each of the 4 subdivisions equals 1,000 g ÷ 4 = 250 g.', 'explanation': '1 kg = 1,000 g. 4 equal spaces mean each mark represents 1,000 ÷ 4 = 250 g. 3 marks past 4 kg = 4 kg + (3 × 250 g) = 4 kg 750 g (or 4 3/4 kg).', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Determine the value of one dial division:</p>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>Interval = 5 kg - 4 kg = 1 kg = 1,000 g<br>1 subdivision = 1,000 g ÷ 4 = 250 g</div>\n                    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Add 3 subdivisions to 4 kg:</p>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-emerald-300'>4 kg + (3 × 250 g) = 4 kg + 750 g = 4 kg 750 g</div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 4 kg 750 g</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q3', 'question': 'On a double number line connecting kilograms and grams, what gram value aligns exactly with 3 1/2 kg?', 'options': ['3,050 g', '3,250 g', '3,500 g', '3,750 g'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': '3 1/2 kg = 3 kg + 1/2 kg. We know 1/2 kg = 500 g.', 'explanation': '3 kg = 3,000 g. 1/2 kg = 500 g. Adding them gives 3,000 + 500 = 3,500 g.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        3 1/2 kg = 3 kg + 0.5 kg<br>\n                        3 kg = 3 × 1,000 g = 3,000 g<br>\n                        0.5 kg = 0.5 × 1,000 g = 500 g<br>\n                        Total = 3,000 g + 500 g = 3,500 g\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 3,500 g</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q4', 'question': 'A grocer weighs a bag of pulses as 5 kg 8 g. What is this mass expressed in total grams?', 'options': ['5,800 g', '5,080 g', '5,008 g', '580 g'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': '8 grams requires two leading zeros in the three-digit gram placeholder: 008.', 'explanation': '5 kg = 5,000 g. 8 g has 0 hundreds and 0 tens, so it is 008 g. Total = 5,000 + 8 = 5,008 g. 5,800 g would be 5 kg 800 g.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <p><strong>Three-Digit Placeholder Rule:</strong></p>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        5 kg = 5,000 g<br>\n                        8 g = 008 g<br>\n                        5,000 g + 008 g = 5,008 g\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-amber-300'>Note: 5,800 g is 5 kg 800 g; 5,080 g is 5 kg 80 g.</p>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 5,008 g</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q5', 'question': 'Which of the following mathematical inequalities is TRUE?', 'options': ['2 kg 70 g > 2,700 g', '3 kg 450 g < 3,045 g', '4 kg 500 g = 4,500 g', '1 kg 900 g > 2 kg'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': 'Convert both sides to grams: 4 kg 500 g = 4,500 g.', 'explanation': '4 kg 500 g = 4,000 + 500 = 4,500 g, which equals 4,500 g. In option A, 2,070 g is NOT > 2,700 g. In option B, 3,450 g is NOT < 3,045 g.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-slate-300 space-y-1'>\n                        <div>• 2 kg 70 g = 2,070 g (< 2,700 g) ➔ False</div>\n                        <div>• 3 kg 450 g = 3,450 g (> 3,045 g) ➔ False</div>\n                        <div>• 4 kg 500 g = 4,500 g (= 4,500 g) ➔ <strong>TRUE</strong></div>\n                        <div>• 1 kg 900 g = 1,900 g (< 2,000 g) ➔ False</div>\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 4 kg 500 g = 4,500 g</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q6', 'question': 'Convert 7,060 grams into compound units of kilograms and grams:', 'options': ['70 kg 60 g', '7 kg 600 g', '7 kg 60 g', '7 kg 6 g'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': 'The thousands digit (7) represents kilograms; the remaining 3 digits (060) represent grams.', 'explanation': '7,060 ÷ 1,000 = 7 with remainder 60. So 7,060 g = 7 kg 60 g.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        7,060 g = 7,000 g + 60 g<br>\n                        7,000 g = 7 kg<br>\n                        60 g = 60 g<br>\n                        Result = 7 kg 60 g\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 7 kg 60 g</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q7', 'question': 'Four backpacks weigh: Bag 1 = 3 kg 250 g, Bag 2 = 3,050 g, Bag 3 = 3 kg 400 g, Bag 4 = 3,200 g. What is the correct descending order of their weights?', 'options': ['Bag 3 > Bag 1 > Bag 4 > Bag 2', 'Bag 3 > Bag 4 > Bag 1 > Bag 2', 'Bag 2 > Bag 4 > Bag 1 > Bag 3', 'Bag 1 > Bag 3 > Bag 4 > Bag 2'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Convert all to grams: Bag 1 = 3,250 g, Bag 2 = 3,050 g, Bag 3 = 3,400 g, Bag 4 = 3,200 g.', 'explanation': 'In grams: Bag 3 (3,400 g) > Bag 1 (3,250 g) > Bag 4 (3,200 g) > Bag 2 (3,050 g).', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Bag 1: 3,250 g<br>\n                        Bag 2: 3,050 g<br>\n                        Bag 3: 3,400 g (Heaviest)<br>\n                        Bag 4: 3,200 g<br>\n                        Descending Order: 3,400 g > 3,250 g > 3,200 g > 3,050 g<br>\n                        ➔ Bag 3 > Bag 1 > Bag 4 > Bag 2\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: Bag 3 > Bag 1 > Bag 4 > Bag 2</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q8', 'question': 'A fruit seller uses standard iron weights on a two-pan balance. To balance a 2 kg 750 g pumpkin, which combination of weights should he place on the opposite pan?', 'options': ['One 2 kg weight and one 500 g weight', 'Two 1 kg weights, one 500 g weight, and one 250 g weight', 'One 2 kg weight and three 200 g weights', 'Five 500 g weights'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Find the combination that sums to exactly 2 kg + 750 g = 2,750 g.', 'explanation': 'Two 1 kg weights = 2,000 g. One 500 g + one 250 g = 750 g. Total = 2,000 + 750 = 2,750 g = 2 kg 750 g.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-emerald-300'>\n                        Target = 2 kg 750 g = 2,750 g<br>\n                        Option B: (2 × 1,000 g) + 500 g + 250 g<br>\n                        = 2,000 g + 750 g = 2,750 g (Exact Match!)\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: Two 1 kg weights, one 500 g weight, and one 250 g weight</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q9', 'question': 'How many 50-gram packets of black pepper can be prepared from a 1 kg container of pepper?', 'options': ['10 packets', '20 packets', '25 packets', '50 packets'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Convert 1 kg to grams: 1,000 g. Divide by 50 g.', 'explanation': '1 kg = 1,000 g. Number of packets = 1,000 ÷ 50 = 20 packets.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Total Mass = 1 kg = 1,000 g<br>\n                        Packet Mass = 50 g<br>\n                        Number of Packets = 1,000 ÷ 50 = 20 packets\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 20 packets</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q10', 'question': 'If 1/4 kg of green chillies costs ₹15, what is the cost of 2 kg of green chillies?', 'options': ['₹60', '₹90', '₹120', '₹150'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': 'There are four 1/4 kg units in 1 kg. So 2 kg contains eight 1/4 kg units.', 'explanation': '1 kg = 4 × (1/4 kg) = 4 × ₹15 = ₹60 per kg. For 2 kg, the cost is 2 × ₹60 = ₹120.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Rate for 1/4 kg = ₹15<br>\n                        Rate for 1 kg = 4 × ₹15 = ₹60<br>\n                        Cost of 2 kg = 2 × ₹60 = ₹120\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: ₹120</p>\n                </div>\n                "}]}, {'id': 18, 'title': 'Challenge 2', 'tagline': 'Mastery Challenge 2 • Milligrams, Quintals, Tonnes & Weight Arithmetic', 'lecture': 'Welcome to Mastery Challenge 2. This session focuses on tiny milligram measurements, heavy commercial units like quintals and metric tonnes, and vertical column addition and subtraction of weights with regrouping.', 'content_html': "<p class='text-xs text-slate-300 mb-4'>Test your mastery of micro-units, heavy metric units, and multi-column arithmetic. Answer all 10 questions to unlock the detailed solutions!</p>", 'questions': [{'id': 'q11', 'question': 'Which of the following objects is most appropriately weighed in milligrams (mg)?', 'options': ['A baby elephant', 'A grain of fine table salt', 'A bag of basmati rice', 'A cricket bat'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Milligrams are used for extremely tiny and feather-light objects.', 'explanation': 'A single grain of salt weighs approximately 1 mg. An elephant is weighed in tonnes, rice in kg, and a cricket bat in kg and grams (~1.2 kg).', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <p><strong>Appropriate Units Analysis:</strong></p>\n                    <ul class='list-disc list-inside text-slate-300 space-y-1'>\n                        <li>Baby elephant: ~100 kg (or tonnes)</li>\n                        <li>Cricket bat: ~1 kg 200 g (kg/g)</li>\n                        <li>Rice bag: 25 kg (kg)</li>\n                        <li>Grain of salt: <strong>~1 mg (milligram)</strong></li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: A grain of fine table salt</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q12', 'question': 'A goldsmith crafts a necklace using 12 g 450 mg of 22-carat gold. What is the total mass of the necklace in milligrams?', 'options': ['1,245 mg', '12,450 mg', '124,500 mg', '12.45 mg'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': '1 g = 1,000 mg. Multiply 12 by 1,000 and add 450.', 'explanation': '12 g = 12 × 1,000 mg = 12,000 mg. Adding 450 mg gives 12,000 + 450 = 12,450 mg.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        1 g = 1,000 mg<br>\n                        12 g = 12,000 mg<br>\n                        Total = 12,000 mg + 450 mg = 12,450 mg\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 12,450 mg</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q13', 'question': 'How many quintals of grain are equivalent to exactly 6 metric tonnes?', 'options': ['6 quintals', '60 quintals', '600 quintals', '0.6 quintals'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': '1 metric tonne = 10 quintals.', 'explanation': 'Since 1 metric tonne = 10 quintals, 6 metric tonnes = 6 × 10 = 60 quintals (which also equals 6,000 kg).', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        1 Metric Tonne = 10 Quintals = 1,000 kg<br>\n                        6 Metric Tonnes = 6 × 10 = 60 Quintals<br>\n                        (Check in kg: 60 × 100 kg = 6,000 kg = 6 tonnes)\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 60 quintals</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q14', 'question': 'A cargo ship transports 45 metric tonnes of steel. How many kilograms of steel does this represent?', 'options': ['4,500 kg', '45,000 kg', '450,000 kg', '450 kg'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': '1 metric tonne = 1,000 kg. Multiply 45 by 1,000.', 'explanation': '45 tonnes × 1,000 kg/tonne = 45,000 kg.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        1 Metric Tonne = 1,000 kg<br>\n                        45 Metric Tonnes = 45 × 1,000 kg = 45,000 kg\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 45,000 kg</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q15', 'question': 'Add the following compound masses: 28 kg 650 g + 19 kg 780 g. What is the sum?', 'options': ['47 kg 430 g', '48 kg 430 g', '48 kg 1,430 g', '47 kg 1,430 g'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Add grams: 650 + 780 = 1,430 g. Carry 1 kg (1,000 g) to kilograms.', 'explanation': 'Grams: 650 + 780 = 1,430 g = 1 kg 430 g. Kilograms: 28 + 19 + 1 (carry) = 48 kg. Total = 48 kg 430 g.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Grams Column: 650 g + 780 g = 1,430 g<br>\n                        Regroup: 1,430 g = 1 kg 430 g (Carry 1 kg)<br>\n                        Kilograms Column: 28 kg + 19 kg + 1 kg = 48 kg<br>\n                        Sum = 48 kg 430 g\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 48 kg 430 g</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q16', 'question': 'A vegetable van carries 12 kg 300 g of cauliflower, 8 kg 750 g of carrots, and 15 kg 450 g of cabbage. What is the total mass of the vegetables?', 'options': ['35 kg 500 g', '36 kg 500 g', '36 kg 1,500 g', '35 kg 1,500 g'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Sum all grams: 300 + 750 + 450 = 1,500 g. Regroup 1,000 g as 1 kg.', 'explanation': 'Grams: 300 + 750 + 450 = 1,500 g = 1 kg 500 g. Kilograms: 12 + 8 + 15 + 1 (carry) = 36 kg. Total = 36 kg 500 g.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Grams: 300 + 750 + 450 = 1,500 g = 1 kg 500 g<br>\n                        Kilograms: 12 + 8 + 15 + 1 (carry) = 36 kg<br>\n                        Total = 36 kg 500 g\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 36 kg 500 g</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q17', 'question': 'Subtract: 34 kg 250 g - 18 kg 680 g. What is the result?', 'options': ['16 kg 570 g', '15 kg 570 g', '15 kg 430 g', '16 kg 430 g'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Borrow 1 kg (1,000 g) from 34 kg to make 1,250 g. Subtract 680 g.', 'explanation': 'Borrow 1 kg from 34 kg, leaving 33 kg. Top grams become 250 + 1,000 = 1,250 g. Grams: 1,250 - 680 = 570 g. Kilograms: 33 - 18 = 15 kg. Result = 15 kg 570 g.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Borrow 1 kg from 34 kg ➔ 33 kg and (250 + 1,000) = 1,250 g<br>\n                        Grams: 1,250 g - 680 g = 570 g<br>\n                        Kilograms: 33 kg - 18 kg = 15 kg<br>\n                        Difference = 15 kg 570 g\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 15 kg 570 g</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q18', 'question': 'A 50 kg sack of wheat flour has a small leak. When inspected at the store, it weighs 46 kg 125 g. How much flour leaked out?', 'options': ['4 kg 875 g', '3 kg 875 g', '3 kg 125 g', '4 kg 125 g'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': '50 kg 000 g - 46 kg 125 g. Borrow 1 kg from 50 kg to get 49 kg 1,000 g.', 'explanation': '50 kg 000 g - 46 kg 125 g: Borrow 1 kg ➔ 49 kg 1,000 g. Grams: 1,000 - 125 = 875 g. Kilograms: 49 - 46 = 3 kg. Leaked flour = 3 kg 875 g.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        50 kg 000 g ➔ 49 kg 1,000 g<br>\n                        Grams: 1,000 g - 125 g = 875 g<br>\n                        Kilograms: 49 kg - 46 kg = 3 kg<br>\n                        Leaked mass = 3 kg 875 g\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 3 kg 875 g</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q19', 'question': 'An elephant displaced an amount of river water equal to the weight of 80 bags of stones. If each bag of stones weighs 50 kg, what is the weight of the elephant in metric tonnes?', 'options': ['2 tonnes', '4 tonnes', '8 tonnes', '40 tonnes'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Total mass = 80 × 50 kg = 4,000 kg. 1 tonne = 1,000 kg.', 'explanation': 'Total weight of stones = 80 × 50 kg = 4,000 kg. Since 1 metric tonne = 1,000 kg, 4,000 kg = 4 metric tonnes.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Total Stones = 80 bags × 50 kg/bag = 4,000 kg<br>\n                        Conversion to Tonnes: 4,000 kg ÷ 1,000 = 4 Metric Tonnes\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 4 tonnes</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q20', 'question': 'A truck has a tare weight (empty weight) of 3 tonnes 500 kg. When fully loaded with bricks, it weighs 8 tonnes 200 kg. What is the weight of the bricks loaded on the truck?', 'options': ['4 tonnes 700 kg', '5 tonnes 300 kg', '4 tonnes 300 kg', '5 tonnes 700 kg'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '8 tonnes 200 kg - 3 tonnes 500 kg. Borrow 1 tonne = 1,000 kg.', 'explanation': 'Borrow 1 tonne from 8 tonnes ➔ 7 tonnes 1,200 kg. Kilograms: 1,200 - 500 = 700 kg. Tonnes: 7 - 3 = 4 tonnes. Bricks weight = 4 tonnes 700 kg.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Loaded: 8 t 200 kg ➔ 7 t 1,200 kg<br>\n                        Empty: - 3 t 500 kg<br>\n                        Bricks = 4 t 700 kg (4,700 kg)\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 4 tonnes 700 kg</p>\n                </div>\n                "}]}, {'id': 19, 'title': 'Challenge 3', 'tagline': 'Mastery Challenge 3 • Proportional Scaling, Capacity Basics & Equivalences', 'lecture': 'Welcome to Mastery Challenge 3. In this module, you will practice proportional multiplication and division of weights, understand liquid volume benchmarks, and master litre to millilitre conversions.', 'content_html': "<p class='text-xs text-slate-300 mb-4'>Test your mastery of proportional scaling, scrap pricing, liquid capacity benchmarks, and unit conversions. Answer all 10 questions to unlock the detailed solutions!</p>", 'questions': [{'id': 'q21', 'question': 'A caterer packs 5 hampers. Each hamper contains 2 kg 450 g of festive sweets. What is the total mass of sweets in all 5 hampers?', 'options': ['10 kg 250 g', '12 kg 250 g', '12 kg 450 g', '11 kg 250 g'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Multiply 2,450 g by 5, or multiply 2 kg × 5 and 450 g × 5.', 'explanation': '5 × 2 kg = 10 kg. 5 × 450 g = 2,250 g = 2 kg 250 g. Total = 10 kg + 2 kg 250 g = 12 kg 250 g.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Method 1: 2,450 g × 5 = 12,250 g = 12 kg 250 g<br>\n                        Method 2: (5 × 2 kg) + (5 × 450 g)<br>\n                        = 10 kg + 2,250 g = 10 kg + 2 kg 250 g = 12 kg 250 g\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 12 kg 250 g</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q22', 'question': 'A box containing 6 kg 300 g of mixed dry fruits is distributed equally among 7 children. How much dry fruit does each child receive?', 'options': ['850 g', '900 g', '950 g', '1 kg'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Convert 6 kg 300 g to 6,300 g. Divide 6,300 by 7.', 'explanation': '6 kg 300 g = 6,300 g. 6,300 ÷ 7 = 900 g per child.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Total Dry Fruits = 6 kg 300 g = 6,300 g<br>\n                        Per Child = 6,300 g ÷ 7 = 900 g\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 900 g</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q23', 'question': 'A kabadiwala buys 16 kg of old iron pipes for ₹448. At this same rate per kg, how much will he pay for 25 kg of iron pipes?', 'options': ['₹650', '₹700', '₹725', '₹750'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Find the rate per kg first: ₹448 ÷ 16 kg. Then multiply by 25 kg.', 'explanation': 'Rate per kg = ₹448 ÷ 16 = ₹28 per kg. For 25 kg = 25 × ₹28 = ₹700.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Rate per kg = ₹448 ÷ 16 = ₹28 / kg<br>\n                        Cost of 25 kg = 25 × ₹28 = ₹700\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: ₹700</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q24', 'question': 'Which of the following capacities is equivalent to exactly 3/4 of a litre?', 'options': ['250 mL', '500 mL', '750 mL', '1,750 mL'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': '1 litre = 1,000 mL. Multiply 1,000 by 3/4.', 'explanation': '3/4 of 1,000 mL = (1,000 ÷ 4) × 3 = 250 × 3 = 750 mL.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        1 Litre = 1,000 mL<br>\n                        3/4 L = (3/4) × 1,000 mL = 3 × 250 mL = 750 mL\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 750 mL</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q25', 'question': 'How many 200 mL cups of tea can be served from a 3-litre tea kettle?', 'options': ['12 cups', '15 cups', '18 cups', '20 cups'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Convert 3 L to 3,000 mL. Divide by 200 mL.', 'explanation': '3 L = 3,000 mL. Number of cups = 3,000 ÷ 200 = 15 cups.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Kettle Capacity = 3 L = 3,000 mL<br>\n                        Cup Capacity = 200 mL<br>\n                        Number of Cups = 3,000 ÷ 200 = 15 cups\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 15 cups</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q26', 'question': 'When reading the water level in a laboratory graduated glass cylinder, at which position should your eye be aligned?', 'options': ['Looking down from above at the upper rim of the curved water surface', 'Horizontally level with the lowest bottom point of the concave meniscus', 'Looking up from below the cylinder bottom', 'At any angle because parallax does not affect graduated cylinders'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Always align your eye horizontally with the lowest dip of the curved meniscus.', 'explanation': 'Scientific protocol requires aligning the eye horizontally with the bottom of the curved meniscus to eliminate optical parallax errors.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <p><strong>Scientific Meniscus Reading Convention:</strong></p>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 text-slate-200'>\n                        Water adheres to glass walls, creating a curved concave surface. To prevent parallax distortion, your line of sight must be exactly horizontal with the <strong>lowest central point of the meniscus</strong>.\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: Horizontally level with the lowest bottom point of the concave meniscus</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q27', 'question': 'A milk delivery can contains 5,025 mL of milk. Express this volume in litres and millilitres:', 'options': ['50 L 25 mL', '5 L 250 mL', '5 L 25 mL', '5 L 2.5 mL'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': '5,025 = 5,000 mL + 25 mL.', 'explanation': '5,025 mL = 5,000 mL + 25 mL = 5 L 25 mL (with a leading zero in the hundreds place: 025).', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        5,025 mL = 5,000 mL + 25 mL<br>\n                        5,000 mL = 5 L<br>\n                        25 mL = 25 mL<br>\n                        Result = 5 L 25 mL\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 5 L 25 mL</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q28', 'question': 'A recipe for biryani calls for 2 L 60 mL of vegetable broth. Which numerical value in millilitres represents this exact quantity?', 'options': ['2,600 mL', '2,060 mL', '260 mL', '20,060 mL'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': '2 L = 2,000 mL. 60 mL is written as 060 in the 3-digit placeholder.', 'explanation': '2 L = 2,000 mL. Adding 60 mL gives 2,000 + 60 = 2,060 mL. 2,600 mL would be 2 L 600 mL.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        2 L = 2,000 mL<br>\n                        60 mL = 060 mL<br>\n                        2,000 mL + 060 mL = 2,060 mL\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 2,060 mL</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q29', 'question': 'An athlete tracks daily water intake. She drinks 400 mL at breakfast, 650 mL during morning training, 500 mL at lunch, and 750 mL after evening running. What is her total water intake in litres and millilitres?', 'options': ['2 L 100 mL', '2 L 300 mL', '2 L 200 mL', '1 L 900 mL'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Add all millilitres: 400 + 650 + 500 + 750.', 'explanation': 'Total = 400 + 650 + 500 + 750 = 2,300 mL = 2,000 mL + 300 mL = 2 L 300 mL.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        400 + 650 = 1,050 mL<br>\n                        1,050 + 500 = 1,550 mL<br>\n                        1,550 + 750 = 2,300 mL<br>\n                        2,300 mL = 2 L 300 mL\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 2 L 300 mL</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q30', 'question': 'Which volume is strictly GREATER than 1 litre 80 millilitres?', 'options': ['1,080 mL', '1,008 mL', '1,800 mL', '1,079 mL'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': '1 L 80 mL = 1,080 mL. Find which option exceeds 1,080 mL.', 'explanation': '1 L 80 mL = 1,080 mL. 1,800 mL (which equals 1 L 800 mL) is much greater than 1,080 mL.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        1 L 80 mL = 1,080 mL<br>\n                        • 1,080 mL = 1,080 mL (Equal)<br>\n                        • 1,008 mL < 1,080 mL (Smaller)<br>\n                        • 1,800 mL > 1,080 mL (GREATER by 720 mL!)<br>\n                        • 1,079 mL < 1,080 mL (Smaller)\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 1,800 mL</p>\n                </div>\n                "}]}, {'id': 20, 'title': 'Challenge 4', 'tagline': 'Mastery Challenge 4 • Fuel Logistics, Oil Partitioning & Multi-Step Story Problems', 'lecture': 'Welcome to the final Mastery Challenge of Chapter 8. This concluding assessment challenges you with real-world fuel logistics, multi-stage commercial packaging, and multi-step reasoning problems.', 'content_html': "<p class='text-xs text-slate-300 mb-4'>Congratulations on reaching the final mastery challenge! Solve these 10 multi-step problems to complete the Weight and Capacity chapter.</p>", 'questions': [{'id': 'q31', 'question': 'A family car has a fuel tank capacity of 48 litres. When arriving at a petrol pump, the fuel gauge shows 14 litres 350 millilitres remaining. How much fuel is needed to fill the tank completely?', 'options': ['34 L 650 mL', '33 L 650 mL', '33 L 350 mL', '34 L 350 mL'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Borrow 1 L (1,000 mL) from 48 L to make 47 L 1,000 mL. Subtract 14 L 350 mL.', 'explanation': '48 L 000 mL - 14 L 350 mL: Borrow 1 L ➔ 47 L 1,000 mL. Millilitres: 1,000 - 350 = 650 mL. Litres: 47 - 14 = 33 L. Needed = 33 L 650 mL.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Full Capacity: 48 L 000 mL ➔ 47 L 1,000 mL<br>\n                        Current Fuel: - 14 L 350 mL<br>\n                        Required = 33 L 650 mL\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 33 L 650 mL</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q32', 'question': 'An auto-rickshaw driver buys petrol three times in a week: 8 L 500 mL on Monday, 6 L 750 mL on Wednesday, and 9 L 250 mL on Friday. What is the total volume of petrol purchased?', 'options': ['23 L 500 mL', '24 L 500 mL', '24 L 250 mL', '23 L 750 mL'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Add millilitres: 500 + 750 + 250 = 1,500 mL = 1 L 500 mL. Add litres: 8 + 6 + 9 + 1.', 'explanation': 'Millilitres: 500 + 750 + 250 = 1,500 mL = 1 L 500 mL. Litres: 8 + 6 + 9 + 1 (carry) = 24 L. Total = 24 L 500 mL.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Millilitres: 500 + 750 + 250 = 1,500 mL = 1 L 500 mL<br>\n                        Litres: 8 + 6 + 9 + 1 (carry) = 24 L<br>\n                        Total = 24 L 500 mL\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 24 L 500 mL</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q33', 'question': 'A juice stall owner prepares 16 litres of fresh orange juice. He fills 50 glasses holding 250 mL each. How much juice remains in his dispenser?', 'options': ['2 L 500 mL', '3 L 500 mL', '4 L 500 mL', '1 L 500 mL'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': '50 × 250 mL = 12,500 mL = 12 L 500 mL. Subtract from 16 L.', 'explanation': 'Poured juice = 50 × 250 mL = 12,500 mL = 12 L 500 mL. Remaining juice = 16 L 000 mL - 12 L 500 mL = 3 L 500 mL.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Total Juice = 16 L = 16,000 mL<br>\n                        Juice Served = 50 × 250 mL = 12,500 mL (12 L 500 mL)<br>\n                        Remaining = 16,000 mL - 12,500 mL = 3,500 mL = 3 L 500 mL\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 3 L 500 mL</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q34', 'question': 'A wholesale oil dealer has an 18-litre drum of groundnut oil. He pours it entirely into bottles holding 600 mL each. How many bottles can he fill?', 'options': ['25 bottles', '30 bottles', '35 bottles', '36 bottles'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Convert 18 L to 18,000 mL. Divide 18,000 by 600.', 'explanation': '18 L = 18,000 mL. Number of bottles = 18,000 ÷ 600 = 30 bottles.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Total Oil = 18 L = 18,000 mL<br>\n                        Bottle Size = 600 mL<br>\n                        Bottles Filled = 18,000 ÷ 600 = 30 bottles\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 30 bottles</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q35', 'question': 'A vendor buys a 20-litre container of mango juice for ₹400. He serves the juice in 200 mL cups and sells each cup for ₹8. What is his total profit?', 'options': ['₹300', '₹400', '₹500', '₹800'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Find total cups: 20,000 mL ÷ 200 mL = 100 cups. Revenue = 100 × ₹8 = ₹800. Profit = Revenue - Cost.', 'explanation': 'Total cups = 20,000 ÷ 200 = 100 cups. Total revenue = 100 × ₹8 = ₹800. Profit = ₹800 - ₹400 = ₹400.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Total Volume = 20 L = 20,000 mL<br>\n                        Cups Served = 20,000 ÷ 200 = 100 cups<br>\n                        Total Revenue = 100 × ₹8 = ₹800<br>\n                        Total Cost = ₹400<br>\n                        Profit = ₹800 - ₹400 = ₹400\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: ₹400</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q36', 'question': 'A dairy farmer has 45 L of milk. He sells 18 L 400 mL to a hotel and 14 L 850 mL to a restaurant. How much milk is left with the farmer?', 'options': ['11 L 750 mL', '12 L 250 mL', '11 L 250 mL', '12 L 750 mL'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Add milk sold: 18 L 400 mL + 14 L 850 mL = 33 L 250 mL. Subtract from 45 L.', 'explanation': 'Milk sold = 18 L 400 mL + 14 L 850 mL = 33 L 250 mL. Leftover = 45 L 000 mL - 33 L 250 mL = 11 L 750 mL.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Total Sold = 18 L 400 mL + 14 L 850 mL = 33 L 250 mL<br>\n                        Remaining = 45 L 000 mL - 33 L 250 mL<br>\n                        44 L 1,000 mL - 33 L 250 mL = 11 L 750 mL\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 11 L 750 mL</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q37', 'question': 'A recipe for lemonade calls for 150 mL of lemon juice, 200 mL of sugar syrup, and 1 L 650 mL of chilled water. What is the total volume of lemonade prepared?', 'options': ['1 L 900 mL', '2 L (2,000 mL)', '2 L 100 mL', '2 L 500 mL'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Sum all components: 150 mL + 200 mL + 1,650 mL.', 'explanation': '150 + 200 + 1,650 = 2,000 mL. Since 2,000 mL = 2 L, exactly 2 litres of lemonade is prepared.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Lemon Juice: 150 mL<br>\n                        Sugar Syrup: 200 mL<br>\n                        Water: 1 L 650 mL = 1,650 mL<br>\n                        Total = 150 + 200 + 1,650 = 2,000 mL = 2 L\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 2 L (2,000 mL)</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q38', 'question': 'A village water tanker carries 1,200 litres of water. It fills 4 community storage tanks, each having a capacity of 240 litres. How much water remains in the tanker?', 'options': ['240 L', '260 L', '300 L', '960 L'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Water dispensed = 4 × 240 L = 960 L. Subtract 960 L from 1,200 L.', 'explanation': 'Water filled = 4 × 240 = 960 L. Remaining water = 1,200 - 960 = 240 L.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Water Distributed = 4 × 240 L = 960 L<br>\n                        Remaining in Tanker = 1,200 L - 960 L = 240 L\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 240 L</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q39', 'question': 'A chemist dissolves 750 mg of salt in 250 mL of water. If she wants to make 1 litre of solution with the exact same concentration, how many milligrams of salt must she dissolve?', 'options': ['1,500 mg', '2,250 mg', '3,000 mg (3 g)', '3,750 mg'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': '1 litre is 4 times 250 mL. Multiply 750 mg by 4.', 'explanation': '1 L = 1,000 mL = 4 × 250 mL. Salt required = 4 × 750 mg = 3,000 mg (or 3 g).', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Volume Scaling Factor = 1,000 mL ÷ 250 mL = 4<br>\n                        Salt Required = 4 × 750 mg = 3,000 mg = 3 g\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 3,000 mg (3 g)</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q40', 'question': 'A school kitchen uses 35 kg of wheat flour each week. If they purchase a 1-quintal sack of flour, for how many full weeks will this single sack last, and how many kilograms will remain?', 'options': ['2 full weeks with 30 kg remaining', '3 full weeks with 5 kg remaining', '2 full weeks with 20 kg remaining', '3 full weeks with 10 kg remaining'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '1 quintal = 100 kg. Divide 100 by 35.', 'explanation': '1 quintal = 100 kg. 100 ÷ 35 = 2 with a remainder of 30 (since 2 × 35 = 70 kg, and 100 - 70 = 30 kg). It lasts 2 full weeks with 30 kg left.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        1 Quintal = 100 kg<br>\n                        Weekly Usage = 35 kg<br>\n                        Division: 100 ÷ 35 = 2 quotient with 30 remainder<br>\n                        (2 weeks × 35 kg = 70 kg; 100 kg - 70 kg = 30 kg remaining)\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 2 full weeks with 30 kg remaining</p>\n                </div>\n                "}]}]
